Object.assign(window.IND_FEED_BODY, {"ss-jt.guttila-veena-2":{"src":"story:jt.guttila-veena:scenes:2:text","title":"The Music Teacher and His Best Student · 3 of 10","body":"\"Teacher,\" said Musila one morning, \"I am as good as you are. I want half your position at court.\" Guttila put down the veena. \"You are very good,\" he said. \"You are not as good as I am. Sit down and I will show you why.\" \"No,\" said Musila. \"Let the city decide. Seven days from now. In front of everybody.\"","art":"art/story/sm/jt-guttila-veena.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.guttila-veena|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Music Teacher and His Best Student","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-jt.guttila-veena-3":{"src":"story:jt.guttila-veena:scenes:3:text","title":"The Music Teacher and His Best Student · 4 of 10","body":"Guttila did not sleep much that week. He was older. His hands were older. And he had taught the boy every single thing the boy knew, which meant there was nothing left that would surprise him.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-guttila-veena.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.guttila-veena|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Music Teacher and His Best Student","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-jt.guttila-veena-4":{"src":"story:jt.guttila-veena:scenes:4:text","title":"The Music Teacher and His Best Student · 5 of 10","body":"On the day, the whole city came. The king sat at the front. Musila played first and he played beautifully — not one wrong note, not one hesitation, and the crowd roared for him.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-guttila-veena.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.guttila-veena|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Music Teacher and His Best Student","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-jt.guttila-veena-6":{"src":"story:jt.guttila-veena:scenes:6:text","title":"The Music Teacher and His Best Student · 7 of 10","body":"Guttila reached down and broke one string off his veena. Then he played on the six that were left, and it was better. So he broke another. And another. And every time the instrument got smaller, the playing got larger.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-guttila-veena.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.guttila-veena|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Music Teacher and His Best Student","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-jt.guttila-veena-7":{"src":"story:jt.guttila-veena:scenes:7:text","title":"The Music Teacher and His Best Student · 8 of 10","body":"He finished on one string, in a silence you could have walked on. Nobody in Benares had ever heard anything like it, because there had never been anything like it to hear.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-guttila-veena.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.guttila-veena|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Music Teacher and His Best Student","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-jt.guttila-veena-8":{"src":"story:jt.guttila-veena:scenes:8:text","title":"The Music Teacher and His Best Student · 9 of 10","body":"Musila sat looking at his own perfect instrument with all seven strings on it. Then he came down off the platform and touched his teacher's feet, and Guttila pulled him up by the elbows before he had got all the way there.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-guttila-veena.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.guttila-veena|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Music Teacher and His Best Student","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-jt.three-birds-king-1":{"src":"story:jt.three-birds-king:scenes:1:text","title":"The King Who Asked Three Birds · 2 of 9","body":"They grew up talking. Court birds do. And the king got into the habit of asking them things, because they had heard every conversation in the palace and were not frightened of him, which was more than he could say for his ministers.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-three-birds-king.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.three-birds-king|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The King Who Asked Three Birds","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-jt.three-birds-king-2":{"src":"story:jt.three-birds-king:scenes:2:text","title":"The King Who Asked Three Birds · 3 of 9","body":"One night he could not sleep. He got up and went and sat by the perches and said, \"I want to ask you three something, and I want the true answer, not the polite one. What must a king do?\"","art":"art/story/sm/jt-three-birds-king.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.three-birds-king|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The King Who Asked Three Birds","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-jt.three-birds-king-3":{"src":"story:jt.three-birds-king:scenes:3:text","title":"The King Who Asked Three Birds · 4 of 9","body":"The eldest, an owl, thought about it a long time. \"Never do a thing in a temper,\" he said, \"and never do a thing you would be ashamed to have explained back to you. Do those two and most of the rest looks after itself.\"","art":"art/story/sm/jt-three-birds-king.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.three-birds-king|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The King Who Asked Three Birds","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-jt.three-birds-king-5":{"src":"story:jt.three-birds-king:scenes:5:text","title":"The King Who Asked Three Birds · 6 of 9","body":"Then the king turned to the youngest, who was a small hen and had said nothing at all, and who everybody assumed had nothing to add.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-three-birds-king.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.three-birds-king|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The King Who Asked Three Birds","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-jt.three-birds-king-6":{"src":"story:jt.three-birds-king:scenes:6:text","title":"The King Who Asked Three Birds · 7 of 9","body":"\"Everyone who talks to you,\" said the hen, \"got past the gate. Somebody let them through. Ask about the people who never get past the gate — the ones too far away, too poor, too busy, or too frightened. What happens to them is what your kingdom actually is.\"","art":"art/story/sm/jt-three-birds-king.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.three-birds-king|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The King Who Asked Three Birds","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-jt.three-birds-king-7":{"src":"story:jt.three-birds-king:scenes:7:text","title":"The King Who Asked Three Birds · 8 of 9","body":"The king sat there in the dark for a while. Then he had all three answers written on gold plate and hung in the hall where the ministers had to walk past them every morning, which nobody enjoyed.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-three-birds-king.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.three-birds-king|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The King Who Asked Three Birds","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-jt.trees-together-1":{"src":"story:jt.trees-together:scenes:1:text","title":"The Tree That Stood Alone · 2 of 10","body":"\"Choose where you will grow this year,\" he said. \"And listen: grow together. Stand in the wood with the others, even if the ground is a little poorer and the light is a little less. Do not go off and take a fine open field on your own.\"","art":"art/story/sm/jt-trees-together.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.trees-together|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Stood Alone","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-jt.trees-together-2":{"src":"story:jt.trees-together:scenes:2:text","title":"The Tree That Stood Alone · 3 of 10","body":"Half of them listened. The other half looked out at the open fields — deep soil, full sun, no roots fighting yours, nobody's branches in your face — and thought: he is being old-fashioned.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-trees-together.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.trees-together|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Stood Alone","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-jt.trees-together-3":{"src":"story:jt.trees-together:scenes:3:text","title":"The Tree That Stood Alone · 4 of 10","body":"\"With respect,\" said one of the young ones, \"why would I stand in a crowd on thin ground when there is a whole field going spare? I will be twice your size by autumn.\" And off they went.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-trees-together.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.trees-together|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Stood Alone","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-jt.trees-together-4":{"src":"story:jt.trees-together:scenes:4:text","title":"The Tree That Stood Alone · 5 of 10","body":"And they were right, for a while. All summer the field trees grew fat and glossy and had blossom the wood trees could only look at.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-trees-together.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.trees-together|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Stood Alone","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-jt.trees-together-6":{"src":"story:jt.trees-together:scenes:6:text","title":"The Tree That Stood Alone · 7 of 10","body":"The field trees took the whole wind on their own, one at a time, from root to crown, and by morning they were lying down in the mud with their roots in the air, every last one.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-trees-together.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.trees-together|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Stood Alone","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-jt.trees-together-7":{"src":"story:jt.trees-together:scenes:7:text","title":"The Tree That Stood Alone · 8 of 10","body":"In the wood the wind hit the outside trees, and they leaned, and their branches locked into the branches of the next ones in, and the whole wood took it together and gave the wind nowhere to get a grip. Some leaves came off. That was all.","art":"art/story/sm/jt-trees-together.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.trees-together|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Stood Alone","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-jt.trees-together-8":{"src":"story:jt.trees-together:scenes:8:text","title":"The Tree That Stood Alone · 9 of 10","body":"\"It was never about the soil,\" said the old king, walking through afterwards. \"You each thought the others were in your way. You were each other's shelter and none of you knew it until Tuesday.\"","art":"art/story/sm/jt-trees-together.jpg","route":"#/story/jt.trees-together|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree That Stood Alone","facts":[["From","More Jataka Tales"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.mongoose-baby-1":{"src":"story:pt.mongoose-baby:scenes:1:text","title":"The Mongoose at the Door · 2 of 10","body":"\"I do not like it,\" said the mother sometimes. \"He is a wild animal.\" \"He is a very small wild animal,\" said the father, \"who has never once been anything but gentle. Look at him.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-mongoose-baby.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.mongoose-baby|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mongoose at the Door","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.mongoose-baby-2":{"src":"story:pt.mongoose-baby:scenes:2:text","title":"The Mongoose at the Door · 3 of 10","body":"One morning she had to go to the river and the baby was asleep. \"Watch him,\" she told her husband, and left. And then her husband was called away too, and he looked at the mongoose sitting by the cradle, and thought: well. He is there.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-mongoose-baby.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.mongoose-baby|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mongoose at the Door","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.mongoose-baby-3":{"src":"story:pt.mongoose-baby:scenes:3:text","title":"The Mongoose at the Door · 4 of 10","body":"He came back at midday. The mongoose met him at the doorway, running, delighted to see him — and his whiskers and his little chest were bright red.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-mongoose-baby.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.mongoose-baby|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mongoose at the Door","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.mongoose-baby-4":{"src":"story:pt.mongoose-baby:scenes:4:text","title":"The Mongoose at the Door · 5 of 10","body":"Something went through the man that was faster than thinking, and it was made entirely of the worst thing he could imagine. His hand came up with the stick still in it.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-mongoose-baby.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.mongoose-baby|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mongoose at the Door","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.mongoose-baby-6":{"src":"story:pt.mongoose-baby:scenes:6:text","title":"The Mongoose at the Door · 7 of 10","body":"He went in. His son was lying in the cradle kicking both legs. And beside the cradle, on the floor, was a snake — dead, and very much the worse for having met a mongoose.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-mongoose-baby.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.mongoose-baby|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mongoose at the Door","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.mongoose-baby-7":{"src":"story:pt.mongoose-baby:scenes:7:text","title":"The Mongoose at the Door · 8 of 10","body":"The man sat down on the floor rather suddenly. The mongoose came and put his front feet on the man's knee, still pleased with himself, still waiting to be told he had done well.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-mongoose-baby.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.mongoose-baby|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mongoose at the Door","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.mongoose-baby-8":{"src":"story:pt.mongoose-baby:scenes:8:text","title":"The Mongoose at the Door · 9 of 10","body":"\"You did,\" said the man, when he could speak. \"You did do well.\" And he did not put that stick down for a long time, because his hands would not stop shaking.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-mongoose-baby.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.mongoose-baby|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mongoose at the Door","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.four-friends-1":{"src":"story:pt.four-friends:scenes:1:text","title":"Four Friends and One Hunter · 2 of 11","body":"One evening a deer came crashing through the reeds with his sides heaving. \"Dogs,\" he said. \"Behind me. Hunters.\" \"Sit down,\" said the tortoise. \"There is water here, there is grass, and there are three of us. Stay.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-four-friends.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.four-friends|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Four Friends and One Hunter","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pt.four-friends-2":{"src":"story:pt.four-friends:scenes:2:text","title":"Four Friends and One Hunter · 3 of 11","body":"His name was Chitranga, and he stayed, and there were four of them after that. They met every day at the same rock. The crow told them what he had seen from the air, which was everything.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-four-friends.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.four-friends|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Four Friends and One Hunter","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pt.four-friends-3":{"src":"story:pt.four-friends:scenes:3:text","title":"Four Friends and One Hunter · 4 of 11","body":"Then one afternoon Chitranga did not come. The crow went up, circled twice, and came down fast. \"He is in a net,\" he said. \"Half a mile east. Rope net, pegged down. And there is a hunter coming back for him at dusk.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-four-friends.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.four-friends|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Four Friends and One Hunter","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pt.four-friends-4":{"src":"story:pt.four-friends:scenes:4:text","title":"Four Friends and One Hunter · 5 of 11","body":"Four friends. One net. A crow who cannot chew rope, a tortoise who cannot hurry, and a mouse who can do both jobs except the getting there.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-four-friends.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.four-friends|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Four Friends and One Hunter","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pt.four-friends-6":{"src":"story:pt.four-friends:scenes:6:text","title":"Four Friends and One Hunter · 7 of 11","body":"And Mantharaka the tortoise — who had been told very clearly to stay at the lake, because he was slow and could not hide and was no use in a hurry — set off after them anyway. Because his friend was in a net.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-four-friends.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.four-friends|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Four Friends and One Hunter","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pt.four-friends-7":{"src":"story:pt.four-friends:scenes:7:text","title":"Four Friends and One Hunter · 8 of 11","body":"The mouse chewed. Rope by rope, knot by knot, faster than seemed possible. The last strand went and Chitranga came out of that net like a spring uncoiling — and at exactly that moment, over the ridge, came the hunter.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-four-friends.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.four-friends|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Four Friends and One Hunter","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pt.four-friends-8":{"src":"story:pt.four-friends:scenes:8:text","title":"Four Friends and One Hunter · 9 of 11","body":"The deer ran. The crow flew. The mouse went down a crack in the ground. And there in the middle of the empty field, twenty paces from the hunter and going as fast as he possibly could, was one tortoise.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-four-friends.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.four-friends|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Four Friends and One Hunter","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pt.four-friends-9":{"src":"story:pt.four-friends:scenes:9:text","title":"Four Friends and One Hunter · 10 of 11","body":"\"Right,\" said Chitranga, and turned round. He limped out in front of the hunter, dragging one leg beautifully, and let himself be chased just fast enough to be worth chasing — a mile, then two, away over the hill. Behind them the mouse chewed the tortoise's sack open, and everyone got home.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-four-friends.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.four-friends|s9","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Four Friends and One Hunter","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pt.mice-iron-1":{"src":"story:pt.mice-iron:scenes:1:text","title":"The Mice Who Ate the Iron · 2 of 9","body":"\"Keep this for me,\" he said to another merchant, an old friend of the family. \"I will be back for it.\" \"Of course,\" said the friend, hefting it and thinking privately about what iron was fetching that season.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-mice-iron.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.mice-iron|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mice Who Ate the Iron","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.mice-iron-2":{"src":"story:pt.mice-iron:scenes:2:text","title":"The Mice Who Ate the Iron · 3 of 9","body":"Years later the first merchant came back, having done well, and asked for his beam. His friend looked at the floor and made his voice go sad. \"Oh, my brother. The mice ate it. Every gram. I am so ashamed.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-mice-iron.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.mice-iron|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mice Who Ate the Iron","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.mice-iron-3":{"src":"story:pt.mice-iron:scenes:3:text","title":"The Mice Who Ate the Iron · 4 of 9","body":"The merchant did not shout. He did not argue. He said, \"Mice. Well. Mice will do that,\" and then he said, \"Send your boy down to the river with me tomorrow — I am going to bathe, and I would like the company.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-mice-iron.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.mice-iron|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mice Who Ate the Iron","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.mice-iron-5":{"src":"story:pt.mice-iron:scenes:5:text","title":"The Mice Who Ate the Iron · 6 of 9","body":"\"Where is my son?\" \"The most terrible thing,\" said the merchant. \"A hawk came down out of the sky and carried him off.\" \"A HAWK? Carried off a boy of twelve? Do you take me for a fool?\" \"Not at all,\" said the merchant. \"Where mice eat iron, hawks carry boys.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-mice-iron.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.mice-iron|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mice Who Ate the Iron","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.mice-iron-6":{"src":"story:pt.mice-iron:scenes:6:text","title":"The Mice Who Ate the Iron · 7 of 9","body":"They ended up in front of the judges, both of them, telling their impossible stories to a room full of people trying not to laugh.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-mice-iron.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.mice-iron|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mice Who Ate the Iron","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.mice-iron-7":{"src":"story:pt.mice-iron:scenes:7:text","title":"The Mice Who Ate the Iron · 8 of 9","body":"The beam came out from under a pile of sacks in the storeroom, entirely un-eaten, with not a single tooth-mark on it anywhere.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-mice-iron.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.mice-iron|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mice Who Ate the Iron","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.crane-crab-1":{"src":"story:pt.crane-crab:scenes:1:text","title":"The Crane Who Offered a Lift · 2 of 10","body":"Then he stopped trying to catch fish, and started standing very still with his head down, looking, to anyone watching from underwater, like a bird in the deepest possible sorrow.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-crane-crab.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.crane-crab|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Crane Who Offered a Lift","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.crane-crab-2":{"src":"story:pt.crane-crab:scenes:2:text","title":"The Crane Who Offered a Lift · 3 of 10","body":"\"What is wrong?\" asked a fish, eventually. \"I overheard the fishermen,\" said the crane, without looking up. \"They are coming to net this pond next week. Every one of you. I have known you all my life and there is nothing I can do.\" And he sighed enormously.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-crane-crab.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.crane-crab|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Crane Who Offered a Lift","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.crane-crab-3":{"src":"story:pt.crane-crab:scenes:3:text","title":"The Crane Who Offered a Lift · 4 of 10","body":"The pond went into a panic. The old fish came in a delegation. \"You fly,\" they said. \"You must know somewhere.\" \"There is a deep pool over the hill,\" admitted the crane. \"I could carry you. Two at a time. It would take me weeks and I am not as strong as I was — but I will do it.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-crane-crab.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.crane-crab|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Crane Who Offered a Lift","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.crane-crab-4":{"src":"story:pt.crane-crab:scenes:4:text","title":"The Crane Who Offered a Lift · 5 of 10","body":"And so it began. Two fish a trip, all day, every day. He flew off over the trees with them and came back empty and tired and dignified. The fish queued up. They argued about whose turn was next.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-crane-crab.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.crane-crab|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Crane Who Offered a Lift","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.crane-crab-6":{"src":"story:pt.crane-crab:scenes:6:text","title":"The Crane Who Offered a Lift · 7 of 10","body":"\"Take me next,\" said the crab. \"But I am heavy and slippery, and if you carry me in your beak I will fall. Let me ride on your neck and hold on.\" \"Naturally,\" said the crane, who was thinking about lunch.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-crane-crab.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.crane-crab|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Crane Who Offered a Lift","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.crane-crab-7":{"src":"story:pt.crane-crab:scenes:7:text","title":"The Crane Who Offered a Lift · 8 of 10","body":"They flew over the hill. There was no deep pool. There was a flat rock in the sun with a great many fish bones on it. The crab looked down at the bones and tightened his claws very slightly, and said, \"Turn round.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-crane-crab.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.crane-crab|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Crane Who Offered a Lift","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.crane-crab-8":{"src":"story:pt.crane-crab:scenes:8:text","title":"The Crane Who Offered a Lift · 9 of 10","body":"The crane turned round. He flew all the way back, very carefully, and set the crab down in the shallows of his own pond, and then he went and stood somewhere else for a long time.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-crane-crab.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.crane-crab|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Crane Who Offered a Lift","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.elephants-rabbits-1":{"src":"story:pt.elephants-rabbits:scenes:1:text","title":"The Rabbit Who Spoke for the Moon · 2 of 10","body":"The elephant herd came down at dawn. They were not being cruel. They were enormously thirsty and they simply walked, and burrows collapsed under them, and it took the rabbits all morning to dig each other out.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-elephants-rabbits.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.elephants-rabbits|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rabbit Who Spoke for the Moon","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.elephants-rabbits-2":{"src":"story:pt.elephants-rabbits:scenes:2:text","title":"The Rabbit Who Spoke for the Moon · 3 of 10","body":"\"They will come again tomorrow,\" said the rabbits. \"And the day after. We cannot fight them and we cannot dig faster than they can walk.\" A young rabbit called Lambakarna — long-ears — stood up at the back. \"Then let me go and talk to them.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-elephants-rabbits.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.elephants-rabbits|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rabbit Who Spoke for the Moon","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.elephants-rabbits-3":{"src":"story:pt.elephants-rabbits:scenes:3:text","title":"The Rabbit Who Spoke for the Moon · 4 of 10","body":"He climbed up onto a high rock near the path, where an elephant could see him without having to look down, and he waited for the herd, and he thought very hard about what a rabbit could possibly say to a thousand elephants.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-elephants-rabbits.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.elephants-rabbits|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rabbit Who Spoke for the Moon","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.elephants-rabbits-4":{"src":"story:pt.elephants-rabbits:scenes:4:text","title":"The Rabbit Who Spoke for the Moon · 5 of 10","body":"\"STOP,\" called Lambakarna, in the biggest voice he had. \"I am the messenger of the moon, and I am to tell you that Moon Lake is hers, and that you have been trampling the ground where her rabbits live.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-elephants-rabbits.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.elephants-rabbits|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rabbit Who Spoke for the Moon","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.elephants-rabbits-6":{"src":"story:pt.elephants-rabbits:scenes:6:text","title":"The Rabbit Who Spoke for the Moon · 7 of 10","body":"That night the herd came down to the water in the dark. The lake was flat as glass, and the full moon lay on it, perfect and enormous and white.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-elephants-rabbits.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.elephants-rabbits|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rabbit Who Spoke for the Moon","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.elephants-rabbits-7":{"src":"story:pt.elephants-rabbits:scenes:7:text","title":"The Rabbit Who Spoke for the Moon · 8 of 10","body":"\"Bow,\" said Lambakarna, \"and touch the water with your trunk, and beg her pardon.\" Chaturdanta lowered his trunk and touched the surface — and the water broke, and the moon shattered into a hundred shivering pieces all over the lake.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-elephants-rabbits.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.elephants-rabbits|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rabbit Who Spoke for the Moon","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.elephants-rabbits-8":{"src":"story:pt.elephants-rabbits:scenes:8:text","title":"The Rabbit Who Spoke for the Moon · 9 of 10","body":"\"She is FURIOUS,\" whispered Lambakarna. Chaturdanta backed away from that water so fast he sat down. The whole herd went off north before dawn and drank from the river instead, which had been there the entire time.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-elephants-rabbits.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.elephants-rabbits|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rabbit Who Spoke for the Moon","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.two-headed-bird-1":{"src":"story:pt.two-headed-bird:scenes:1:text","title":"The Bird With Two Heads · 2 of 8","body":"One day the right head, poking about in the driftwood, found a fruit — a red one, the sweet kind, the kind you get about twice a year. It ate it slowly, making a great deal of noise about how good it was.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-two-headed-bird.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.two-headed-bird|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bird With Two Heads","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.two-headed-bird-2":{"src":"story:pt.two-headed-bird:scenes:2:text","title":"The Bird With Two Heads · 3 of 8","body":"\"Give me a piece,\" said the left head. \"Half a piece. A taste.\" \"Why?\" said the right head, chewing. \"It goes into the same stomach either way. You will get it just the same. What difference does it make?\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-two-headed-bird.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.two-headed-bird|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bird With Two Heads","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.two-headed-bird-3":{"src":"story:pt.two-headed-bird:scenes:3:text","title":"The Bird With Two Heads · 4 of 8","body":"And that was true, and it was also completely beside the point, and the left head went quiet in a way that lasted for weeks.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-two-headed-bird.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.two-headed-bird|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bird With Two Heads","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.two-headed-bird-5":{"src":"story:pt.two-headed-bird:scenes:5:text","title":"The Bird With Two Heads · 6 of 8","body":"\"Do not,\" said the right head, suddenly frightened. \"That will kill us both.\" \"Us,\" said the left head. \"How interesting. It was ME when there was fruit and US when there is trouble.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-two-headed-bird.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.two-headed-bird|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bird With Two Heads","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.two-headed-bird-6":{"src":"story:pt.two-headed-bird:scenes:6:text","title":"The Bird With Two Heads · 7 of 8","body":"It ate the grey fruit. And of course it went to the same stomach, because there was only ever one stomach — and the bird with two heads came down on the sand and did not get up.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-two-headed-bird.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.two-headed-bird|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bird With Two Heads","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.lion-makers-1":{"src":"story:pt.lion-makers:scenes:1:text","title":"The Four Clever Men and the Lion · 2 of 10","body":"\"What is the point of him coming?\" said the first. \"He has no learning.\" \"Let him come,\" said the third. \"He is a friend, and friendship is worth something.\" \"Not as much as learning,\" said the second, and they walked on.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-lion-makers.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.lion-makers|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Four Clever Men and the Lion","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.lion-makers-2":{"src":"story:pt.lion-makers:scenes:2:text","title":"The Four Clever Men and the Lion · 3 of 10","body":"Halfway through a forest they came upon a heap of old bones lying in a clearing — a big animal, long dead, bleached white and scattered about.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-lion-makers.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.lion-makers|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Four Clever Men and the Lion","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.lion-makers-3":{"src":"story:pt.lion-makers:scenes:3:text","title":"The Four Clever Men and the Lion · 4 of 10","body":"\"Here is our chance to show what we can do,\" said the first. \"I can put these bones back together.\" He did. It took an hour, and at the end of it there was a complete skeleton standing in the clearing.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-lion-makers.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.lion-makers|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Four Clever Men and the Lion","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.lion-makers-4":{"src":"story:pt.lion-makers:scenes:4:text","title":"The Four Clever Men and the Lion · 5 of 10","body":"\"I can clothe it in flesh and skin,\" said the second — and did, and now there was a lion lying in the grass, whole and perfect and entirely still. \"And I,\" said the third, rolling up his sleeves, \"can give it life.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-lion-makers.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.lion-makers|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Four Clever Men and the Lion","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.lion-makers-6":{"src":"story:pt.lion-makers:scenes:6:text","title":"The Four Clever Men and the Lion · 7 of 10","body":"\"It is a lion,\" said the fourth man. \"That is a lion. I know I did not study, but I have seen one. Give me a moment.\" And he went up the nearest tree like a squirrel and sat on a high branch with his arms round the trunk.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-lion-makers.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.lion-makers|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Four Clever Men and the Lion","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.lion-makers-7":{"src":"story:pt.lion-makers:scenes:7:text","title":"The Four Clever Men and the Lion · 8 of 10","body":"The third man finished his work. The lion opened its eyes, stood up, stretched every leg in turn, and roared — and the three cleverest young men in that province were over the hill and out of sight before it had finished.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-lion-makers.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.lion-makers|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Four Clever Men and the Lion","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.lion-makers-8":{"src":"story:pt.lion-makers:scenes:8:text","title":"The Four Clever Men and the Lion · 9 of 10","body":"The lion looked round the empty clearing, yawned, and wandered off into the trees to find out what a forest was. It had, after all, only been alive for about a minute.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-lion-makers.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.lion-makers|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Four Clever Men and the Lion","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pt.drum-jackal-1":{"src":"story:pt.drum-jackal:scenes:1:text","title":"The Thing That Boomed in the Bushes · 2 of 8","body":"And out of a clump of bushes at the far end came a noise that stopped his heart: BOOM. Then nothing. Then BOOM again, deeper.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-drum-jackal.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.drum-jackal|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Thing That Boomed in the Bushes","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.drum-jackal-2":{"src":"story:pt.drum-jackal:scenes:2:text","title":"The Thing That Boomed in the Bushes · 3 of 8","body":"\"That,\" said Gomaya to himself, backing away, \"is enormous. Anything that makes a noise like that could eat me sideways. Home. Now.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-drum-jackal.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.drum-jackal|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Thing That Boomed in the Bushes","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.drum-jackal-3":{"src":"story:pt.drum-jackal:scenes:3:text","title":"The Thing That Boomed in the Bushes · 4 of 8","body":"He got about thirty paces. Then he stopped, because he had noticed something. The booming only happened when the wind came across the field. When the wind dropped, the enormous terrible thing said nothing at all.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-drum-jackal.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.drum-jackal|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Thing That Boomed in the Bushes","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.drum-jackal-5":{"src":"story:pt.drum-jackal:scenes:5:text","title":"The Thing That Boomed in the Bushes · 6 of 8","body":"In the bushes lay a war drum, abandoned when the army broke camp, taller than he was and lying on its side. A branch swung over it in the wind and hit the skin. BOOM. He watched it do it three times.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-drum-jackal.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.drum-jackal|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Thing That Boomed in the Bushes","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.drum-jackal-6":{"src":"story:pt.drum-jackal:scenes:6:text","title":"The Thing That Boomed in the Bushes · 7 of 8","body":"He walked round it, sniffed it, and then found what the drum had been hiding: a whole sack of the army's food, dropped and forgotten, which Gomaya ate over the next four days in a very good mood indeed.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-drum-jackal.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.drum-jackal|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Thing That Boomed in the Bushes","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.monkey-wedge-1":{"src":"story:pt.monkey-wedge:scenes:1:text","title":"The Monkey and the Wedge · 2 of 8","body":"A troop of them had been watching from the wall for days. They had seen the saws and the chisels and the mallets, and had understood, roughly, that the men were making wood into other shapes, which seemed a reasonable enough way to spend a life.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-monkey-wedge.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.monkey-wedge|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Monkey and the Wedge","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.monkey-wedge-2":{"src":"story:pt.monkey-wedge:scenes:2:text","title":"The Monkey and the Wedge · 3 of 8","body":"At noon the carpenters put down their tools and went off to eat. They left a great log half sawn down the middle, with a wooden wedge hammered into the split to hold the two halves apart while they worked.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-monkey-wedge.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.monkey-wedge|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Monkey and the Wedge","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.monkey-wedge-3":{"src":"story:pt.monkey-wedge:scenes:3:text","title":"The Monkey and the Wedge · 4 of 8","body":"The moment the yard was empty, down came the monkeys. They tried the saw. They sat in the bucket. And one young monkey — the busiest and the most helpful of them — sat himself down astride the log, right over the gap, and took hold of the wedge.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-monkey-wedge.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.monkey-wedge|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Monkey and the Wedge","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.monkey-wedge-5":{"src":"story:pt.monkey-wedge:scenes:5:text","title":"The Monkey and the Wedge · 6 of 8","body":"He got both hands on it and heaved. It shifted. He heaved again — and the wedge came out, and the two halves of that log came together like a pair of hands clapping, with a considerable portion of monkey tail in the middle.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-monkey-wedge.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.monkey-wedge|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Monkey and the Wedge","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.monkey-wedge-6":{"src":"story:pt.monkey-wedge:scenes:6:text","title":"The Monkey and the Wedge · 7 of 8","body":"The carpenters came back from lunch to a great deal of shouting. They levered the log open, and the monkey went up the wall and over it and did not stop until he was three streets away.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-monkey-wedge.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.monkey-wedge|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Monkey and the Wedge","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.singing-donkey-1":{"src":"story:pt.singing-donkey:scenes:1:text","title":"The Donkey Who Wanted an Audience · 2 of 10","body":"\"I know a cucumber field,\" said the jackal one night. \"Full grown. No dog. No fence to speak of.\" And the two of them went off through the dark together, and the donkey ate more in one hour than he had eaten in the previous fortnight.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-singing-donkey.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.singing-donkey|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Donkey Who Wanted an Audience","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.singing-donkey-2":{"src":"story:pt.singing-donkey:scenes:2:text","title":"The Donkey Who Wanted an Audience · 3 of 10","body":"They went back the next night, and the night after. It was, by any measure, the best week of that donkey's entire life.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-singing-donkey.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.singing-donkey|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Donkey Who Wanted an Audience","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.singing-donkey-3":{"src":"story:pt.singing-donkey:scenes:3:text","title":"The Donkey Who Wanted an Audience · 4 of 10","body":"On the fourth night, full to the eyebrows, Uddhata stopped chewing and gazed at the moon. \"Nephew,\" he said. \"The night is beautiful. I feel that I would like to sing.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-singing-donkey.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.singing-donkey|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Donkey Who Wanted an Audience","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.singing-donkey-4":{"src":"story:pt.singing-donkey:scenes:4:text","title":"The Donkey Who Wanted an Audience · 5 of 10","body":"\"Uncle,\" said the jackal, very carefully, \"we are thieves in a cucumber field. Thieves do not sing.\" \"You have no ear,\" said the donkey. \"That is your trouble. You have no ear at all.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pt-singing-donkey.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.singing-donkey|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Donkey Who Wanted an Audience","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.singing-donkey-6":{"src":"story:pt.singing-donkey:scenes:6:text","title":"The Donkey Who Wanted an Audience · 7 of 10","body":"\"You are quite right, uncle,\" said the jackal. \"Sing. Only let me step outside the fence first — one hears a fine voice much better from a distance.\" And he went through the gap and lay down comfortably on the far side.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-singing-donkey.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.singing-donkey|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Donkey Who Wanted an Audience","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.singing-donkey-7":{"src":"story:pt.singing-donkey:scenes:7:text","title":"The Donkey Who Wanted an Audience · 8 of 10","body":"Uddhata filled his lungs, put his head back, and let go. It was not a song. It was the sound a gate makes when it is being murdered. Lights came on in three houses. The farmer came out of the hut at a dead run.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-singing-donkey.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.singing-donkey|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Donkey Who Wanted an Audience","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pt.singing-donkey-8":{"src":"story:pt.singing-donkey:scenes:8:text","title":"The Donkey Who Wanted an Audience · 9 of 10","body":"The donkey walked home very slowly that night, and got not one word of sympathy from the jackal, who had watched the whole thing from the ditch with his chin on his paws.","art":"art/story/sm/pt-singing-donkey.jpg","route":"#/story/pt.singing-donkey|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Donkey Who Wanted an Audience","facts":[["From","More Panchatantra"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-pu.samudra-manthan-1":{"src":"story:pu.samudra-manthan:scenes:1:text","title":"The Day They Churned the Sea · 2 of 9","body":"The answer, when it came, was extraordinary. Churn the ocean. Not a pot of curd — the ocean. Turn the whole sea over the way you turn milk into butter, and everything that was lost will come up.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-samudra-manthan.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.samudra-manthan|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Day They Churned the Sea","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pu.samudra-manthan-2":{"src":"story:pu.samudra-manthan:scenes:2:text","title":"The Day They Churned the Sea · 3 of 9","body":"They took Mount Mandara for the churning stick and the great serpent Vasuki for the rope, wound round the middle. And because one side could not do it alone, the devas took the tail and the asuras took the head, and for the length of this one job the two of them pulled together.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-samudra-manthan.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.samudra-manthan|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Day They Churned the Sea","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pu.samudra-manthan-3":{"src":"story:pu.samudra-manthan:scenes:3:text","title":"The Day They Churned the Sea · 4 of 9","body":"The mountain began to sink into the seabed almost at once. So Vishnu came as Kurma the tortoise, enormous and patient, and put his back under it — and the churning went on with a mountain spinning on the shell of a turtle in the middle of the sea.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-samudra-manthan.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.samudra-manthan|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Day They Churned the Sea","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pu.samudra-manthan-5":{"src":"story:pu.samudra-manthan:scenes:5:text","title":"The Day They Churned the Sea · 6 of 9","body":"Shiva took the halahala and drank it. And Parvati put her hand at his throat and held it there, so that it went no further down — and it stayed in his throat, and his throat turned dark blue, and that is why he is called Nilakantha, the blue-throated one.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-samudra-manthan.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.samudra-manthan|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Day They Churned the Sea","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pu.samudra-manthan-6":{"src":"story:pu.samudra-manthan:scenes:6:text","title":"The Day They Churned the Sea · 7 of 9","body":"Then the churning went on. And the sea gave up wonder after wonder: the wish-granting cow, the moon, the divine physician, the celestial tree — and Lakshmi herself, standing on a lotus, come up out of the water.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-samudra-manthan.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.samudra-manthan|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Day They Churned the Sea","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pu.samudra-manthan-7":{"src":"story:pu.samudra-manthan:scenes:7:text","title":"The Day They Churned the Sea · 8 of 9","body":"And last of all, carried up in a pot, came amrita — the nectar that ends death. Which is where the two sides on the rope stopped pulling together, and the rest of that story is told a hundred different ways in a hundred different places.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-samudra-manthan.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.samudra-manthan|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Day They Churned the Sea","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pu.ganga-shiva-1":{"src":"story:pu.ganga-shiva:scenes:1:text","title":"Catching a River in Your Hair · 2 of 8","body":"So he went up into the mountains and stood there. Not for a week. Not for a season. For years, in the cold and the thin air, asking for one thing, until the asking wore a groove in the world.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-ganga-shiva.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.ganga-shiva|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Catching a River in Your Hair","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.ganga-shiva-2":{"src":"story:pu.ganga-shiva:scenes:2:text","title":"Catching a River in Your Hair · 3 of 8","body":"\"Let the river come down,\" he said. And at last he was told: it will come. But look at what you are asking for. She falls from the sky. When she lands, she will split the earth open and carry it away, and there will not be anything left to water.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-ganga-shiva.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.ganga-shiva|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Catching a River in Your Hair","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.ganga-shiva-3":{"src":"story:pu.ganga-shiva:scenes:3:text","title":"Catching a River in Your Hair · 4 of 8","body":"And so Bhagiratha went back to standing still, and this time he asked Shiva.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-ganga-shiva.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.ganga-shiva|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Catching a River in Your Hair","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.ganga-shiva-5":{"src":"story:pu.ganga-shiva:scenes:5:text","title":"Catching a River in Your Hair · 6 of 8","body":"She was in there a long time, looking for the way out. When she came, she came as a stream you could stand beside: cold and clear and quick, running down the rock and out onto the plain, going where Bhagiratha walked in front of her.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-ganga-shiva.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.ganga-shiva|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Catching a River in Your Hair","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.ganga-shiva-6":{"src":"story:pu.ganga-shiva:scenes:6:text","title":"Catching a River in Your Hair · 7 of 8","body":"He walked all the way down. Out of the mountains, across the plain, down through the whole length of the country to the sea, with a river following him the entire way — and where he walked is where the Ganga still runs.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-ganga-shiva.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.ganga-shiva|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Catching a River in Your Hair","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.durga-mahisha-1":{"src":"story:pu.durga-mahisha:scenes:1:text","title":"The One They All Made Together · 2 of 9","body":"So he took everything, and there was nothing anybody could do about it, and the devas found themselves standing about in the hills with no heaven and no idea.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-durga-mahisha.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.durga-mahisha|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The One They All Made Together","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.durga-mahisha-2":{"src":"story:pu.durga-mahisha:scenes:2:text","title":"The One They All Made Together · 3 of 9","body":"They sat down together and worked through the wording again, word by word, the way you do with a promise that has gone wrong.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-durga-mahisha.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.durga-mahisha|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The One They All Made Together","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.durga-mahisha-3":{"src":"story:pu.durga-mahisha:scenes:3:text","title":"The One They All Made Together · 4 of 9","body":"And then they did something none of them had ever done. Each one gave away the strongest thing they had. Shiva gave his light. Vishnu gave his. Every deva put their own power into the same place, and it went up like a fire on a hilltop and did not go out.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-durga-mahisha.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.durga-mahisha|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The One They All Made Together","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.durga-mahisha-5":{"src":"story:pu.durga-mahisha:scenes:5:text","title":"The One They All Made Together · 6 of 9","body":"\"You have all given me everything you have,\" she said, looking round at them. \"Good. Now stand back — this part is mine.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pu-durga-mahisha.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.durga-mahisha|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The One They All Made Together","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.durga-mahisha-6":{"src":"story:pu.durga-mahisha:scenes:6:text","title":"The One They All Made Together · 7 of 9","body":"What happened on that battlefield takes nine nights in the telling, which is exactly how long people take over it. Mahishasura changed his shape again and again to get away from her — and on the tenth day it was finished, and the world was handed back.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-durga-mahisha.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.durga-mahisha|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The One They All Made Together","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.durga-mahisha-7":{"src":"story:pu.durga-mahisha:scenes:7:text","title":"The One They All Made Together · 8 of 9","body":"That is why the festival runs nine nights and finishes on the tenth. In Bengal she is Ma Durga coming home to her parents for four days, and the whole city is built into a gallery for her. In Mysuru the palace is lit up and Chamundeshwari goes through the streets. In Gujarat everybody dances for nine nights. In many families it looks different again.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-durga-mahisha.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.durga-mahisha|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The One They All Made Together","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.narasimha-prahlad-1":{"src":"story:pu.narasimha-prahlad:scenes:1:text","title":"The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name · 2 of 11","body":"And with the promise in hand he took everything, and made one rule: nobody in his kingdom was to speak the name of Vishnu. Not in a temple, not in a house, not under their breath. His own name was to be said instead.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-narasimha-prahlad.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.narasimha-prahlad|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pu.narasimha-prahlad-2":{"src":"story:pu.narasimha-prahlad:scenes:2:text","title":"The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name · 3 of 11","body":"His son Prahlad was a small boy who liked the name of Vishnu the way you like a song you cannot get out of your head. He said it while he ate. He said it in his sleep. He taught it to the other children in the schoolroom, and the teachers went pale.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-narasimha-prahlad.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.narasimha-prahlad|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pu.narasimha-prahlad-3":{"src":"story:pu.narasimha-prahlad:scenes:3:text","title":"The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name · 4 of 11","body":"\"You are my son,\" said Hiranyakashipu. \"Say my name.\" \"I love you,\" said Prahlad, \"and I will do anything you ask me. But that name is not something I can put down. It is not in my hand. It is in me.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pu-narasimha-prahlad.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.narasimha-prahlad|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pu.narasimha-prahlad-4":{"src":"story:pu.narasimha-prahlad:scenes:4:text","title":"The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name · 5 of 11","body":"His father tried everything a frightened king tries. He had the boy taken away and frightened and left alone. None of it worked, and none of it is a nice part of the story, and Prahlad came back from every single one of it still saying the name.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-narasimha-prahlad.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.narasimha-prahlad|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pu.narasimha-prahlad-6":{"src":"story:pu.narasimha-prahlad:scenes:6:text","title":"The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name · 7 of 11","body":"\"He is in the pillar,\" said Prahlad. \"And in that door, and in the floor, and in you. There is nowhere he is not. I am sorry, Father. I am not being difficult.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pu-narasimha-prahlad.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.narasimha-prahlad|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pu.narasimha-prahlad-7":{"src":"story:pu.narasimha-prahlad:scenes:7:text","title":"The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name · 8 of 11","body":"Hiranyakashipu struck the pillar with everything he had. And the pillar broke open — and out of it came Narasimha: not a man and not a lion but both at once, which was not on the list.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-narasimha-prahlad.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.narasimha-prahlad|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pu.narasimha-prahlad-8":{"src":"story:pu.narasimha-prahlad:scenes:8:text","title":"The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name · 9 of 11","body":"It was dusk, which is neither day nor night. He carried the king to the threshold of the door, which is neither indoors nor out, and set him across his knees, which is neither ground nor air, and used no weapon at all. Every single word of that promise was kept, and it did not save him.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-narasimha-prahlad.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.narasimha-prahlad|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pu.narasimha-prahlad-9":{"src":"story:pu.narasimha-prahlad:scenes:9:text","title":"The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name · 10 of 11","body":"And then the terrible form stood in the hall with everyone flat on the floor and nobody daring to breathe — until one small boy walked straight up to it, put his hands together, and said hello. And Narasimha grew quiet, and sat down, and put the boy on his knee.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-narasimha-prahlad.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.narasimha-prahlad|s9","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Would Not Stop Saying the Name","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-pu.dhruva-star-1":{"src":"story:pu.dhruva-star:scenes:1:text","title":"The Boy Who Became a Star · 2 of 10","body":"Dhruva was five. He climbed onto his father's lap one afternoon the way any five-year-old does, and was lifted straight off it again. \"That place is not for you,\" he was told, in front of the whole room.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-dhruva-star.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.dhruva-star|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Became a Star","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.dhruva-star-2":{"src":"story:pu.dhruva-star:scenes:2:text","title":"The Boy Who Became a Star · 3 of 10","body":"He went and found his mother, Suniti, and stood in front of her with his chin going. \"Is there anywhere,\" he said, \"where nobody can take me off?\"","art":"art/story/sm/pu-dhruva-star.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.dhruva-star|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Became a Star","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.dhruva-star-3":{"src":"story:pu.dhruva-star:scenes:3:text","title":"The Boy Who Became a Star · 4 of 10","body":"She could have told him it did not matter. She could have told him to be patient. Instead she thought about it seriously, because he had asked her seriously.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-dhruva-star.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.dhruva-star|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Became a Star","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.dhruva-star-4":{"src":"story:pu.dhruva-star:scenes:4:text","title":"The Boy Who Became a Star · 5 of 10","body":"\"There is one lap nobody takes anybody off,\" she said. \"Go and ask for that one.\" And Dhruva, being five, took her completely literally, walked out of the palace and into the forest, and started asking.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-dhruva-star.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.dhruva-star|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Became a Star","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.dhruva-star-6":{"src":"story:pu.dhruva-star:scenes:6:text","title":"The Boy Who Became a Star · 7 of 10","body":"He stood there. Months, the story says. And when Vishnu came at last and stood in front of him, Dhruva looked up and found that he had forgotten every single thing he had come to complain about.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-dhruva-star.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.dhruva-star|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Became a Star","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.dhruva-star-7":{"src":"story:pu.dhruva-star:scenes:7:text","title":"The Boy Who Became a Star · 8 of 10","body":"\"I came here because I was angry,\" he said, \"and I do not know where the anger has gone. I do not want a lap any more. I would just like to be able to say your name properly.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pu-dhruva-star.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.dhruva-star|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Became a Star","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.dhruva-star-8":{"src":"story:pu.dhruva-star:scenes:8:text","title":"The Boy Who Became a Star · 9 of 10","body":"He went home. He was a good king for a long time. And when it was finished, they say he was set in the northern sky at the one point that does not move — the star everything else turns around, the one sailors and travellers steer by. In India it is called Dhruva Tara.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-dhruva-star.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.dhruva-star|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Became a Star","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.krishna-kaliya-1":{"src":"story:pu.krishna-kaliya:scenes:1:text","title":"The Boy Who Danced on the Snake · 2 of 10","body":"Kaliya lived in that pool. He was a serpent with a great many heads and a temper to match, and he had come there running from something bigger than himself, and had stayed, and had poisoned the whole bend of the river without particularly meaning to.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-krishna-kaliya.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.krishna-kaliya|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Danced on the Snake","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.krishna-kaliya-2":{"src":"story:pu.krishna-kaliya:scenes:2:text","title":"The Boy Who Danced on the Snake · 3 of 10","body":"One afternoon the boys were playing ball on the bank, and somebody threw it badly, and it went out over the black water and down. And Krishna, who was a boy at this point in the story and behaved like one, went straight up a kadamba tree on the bank.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-krishna-kaliya.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.krishna-kaliya|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Danced on the Snake","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.krishna-kaliya-3":{"src":"story:pu.krishna-kaliya:scenes:3:text","title":"The Boy Who Danced on the Snake · 4 of 10","body":"The other boys shouted at him from the grass. He looked down at the water, and then at the dead trees, and then along the bank at the whole village that had lost a river.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-krishna-kaliya.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.krishna-kaliya|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Danced on the Snake","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.krishna-kaliya-4":{"src":"story:pu.krishna-kaliya:scenes:4:text","title":"The Boy Who Danced on the Snake · 5 of 10","body":"He jumped. The water closed over him and the boys ran for the village screaming, and by the time the whole of Vrindavan was on the bank the surface was churning like a pot on a fire and there was no sign of anybody.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-krishna-kaliya.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.krishna-kaliya|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Danced on the Snake","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.krishna-kaliya-6":{"src":"story:pu.krishna-kaliya:scenes:6:text","title":"The Boy Who Danced on the Snake · 7 of 10","body":"He danced across them one to the next, and every time a head came up he was already on it and it went down again, and the river went white and the whole village stood in the shallows and watched.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-krishna-kaliya.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.krishna-kaliya|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Danced on the Snake","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.krishna-kaliya-7":{"src":"story:pu.krishna-kaliya:scenes:7:text","title":"The Boy Who Danced on the Snake · 8 of 10","body":"And then, from the water, came a voice that nobody expected: the wives of Kaliya, with their hands together. \"He did not come here to be wicked. He came here because he was hunted. Do not finish him. Send him away.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pu-krishna-kaliya.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.krishna-kaliya|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Danced on the Snake","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.krishna-kaliya-8":{"src":"story:pu.krishna-kaliya:scenes:8:text","title":"The Boy Who Danced on the Snake · 9 of 10","body":"Krishna stopped dancing. \"Go and live in the sea,\" he said. \"You will not be hunted there. And do not come back to this river.\" And Kaliya went — bruised, and alive, and carrying, they say, the print of a boy's foot on his head so that nothing would ever trouble him again.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-krishna-kaliya.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.krishna-kaliya|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Danced on the Snake","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.govardhan-1":{"src":"story:pu.govardhan:scenes:1:text","title":"The Hill That Became an Umbrella · 2 of 10","body":"\"Can I ask something?\" said Krishna, who was young and had a way of asking things at awkward moments. \"Where do the cows eat?\" \"On Govardhan hill.\" \"Where does the grass come from?\" \"The hill.\" \"And who has stood over us our whole lives, giving us grass and water and firewood and shade?\"","art":"art/story/sm/pu-govardhan.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.govardhan|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Hill That Became an Umbrella","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.govardhan-2":{"src":"story:pu.govardhan:scenes:2:text","title":"The Hill That Became an Umbrella · 3 of 10","body":"The elders looked at each other. \"The hill,\" said one of them slowly. \"Then offer it to the hill,\" said Krishna. \"Take the food to Govardhan. Walk round it. Thank the thing that is actually feeding you.\"","art":"art/story/sm/pu-govardhan.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.govardhan|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Hill That Became an Umbrella","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.govardhan-3":{"src":"story:pu.govardhan:scenes:3:text","title":"The Hill That Became an Umbrella · 4 of 10","body":"And, astonishingly, they did. The whole village carried the feast up to the hill and walked round it singing, and it was the best day anybody could remember — right up until the sky went the colour of iron.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-govardhan.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.govardhan|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Hill That Became an Umbrella","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.govardhan-4":{"src":"story:pu.govardhan:scenes:4:text","title":"The Hill That Became an Umbrella · 5 of 10","body":"Indra, who had had that offering every year since anyone could remember, sent rain. Not ordinary rain. Rain like a river falling sideways, for days, until the fields were gone and the cattle were standing in water up to their knees and the houses began to come apart.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-govardhan.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.govardhan|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Hill That Became an Umbrella","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.govardhan-6":{"src":"story:pu.govardhan:scenes:6:text","title":"The Hill That Became an Umbrella · 7 of 10","body":"\"IN!\" shouted the elders, and the whole of Vrindavan went in underneath — every person, every cow, every calf, the dogs, the carts, all of it — and stood there in the dry and looked up at a hill.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-govardhan.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.govardhan|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Hill That Became an Umbrella","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.govardhan-7":{"src":"story:pu.govardhan:scenes:7:text","title":"The Hill That Became an Umbrella · 8 of 10","body":"And then the cowherds did something that families still tell each other about. They took their staffs and their sticks, and they went and stood under the rim of that hill and held them up too — all of them, propping it, taking their share of the weight.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-govardhan.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.govardhan|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Hill That Became an Umbrella","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.govardhan-8":{"src":"story:pu.govardhan:scenes:8:text","title":"The Hill That Became an Umbrella · 9 of 10","body":"Seven days it rained. Seven days he held it. When the sky cleared he put the hill back exactly where it had been, and everybody walked out into a wet, bright, completely undamaged morning.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-govardhan.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.govardhan|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Hill That Became an Umbrella","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.markandeya-1":{"src":"story:pu.markandeya:scenes:1:text","title":"The Boy Who Held On to the Stone · 2 of 10","body":"They chose the sixteen years. People argue about that choice and always will. What is certain is that they had him, and they called him Markandeya, and they loved him with everything they had, and they counted.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-markandeya.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.markandeya|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Held On to the Stone","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.markandeya-2":{"src":"story:pu.markandeya:scenes:2:text","title":"The Boy Who Held On to the Stone · 3 of 10","body":"He grew up knowing. Nobody hid it from him. And what he did with the knowing was decide, quite early, that he was going to spend his time on the one thing he actually liked — sitting in the little Shiva shrine by the sea, saying the name, keeping the lamp going.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-markandeya.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.markandeya|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Held On to the Stone","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.markandeya-3":{"src":"story:pu.markandeya:scenes:3:text","title":"The Boy Who Held On to the Stone · 4 of 10","body":"On the morning of his sixteenth birthday his mother could not stop crying and his father could not speak. Markandeya washed, and went down to the shrine as he did every day, and put his arms round the smooth stone lingam and held on to it.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-markandeya.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.markandeya|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Held On to the Stone","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.markandeya-4":{"src":"story:pu.markandeya:scenes:4:text","title":"The Boy Who Held On to the Stone · 5 of 10","body":"And in the afternoon, Yama came for him. Not cruelly — Yama is not cruel, he simply has a job and does it exactly on time.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-markandeya.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.markandeya|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Held On to the Stone","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.markandeya-6":{"src":"story:pu.markandeya:scenes:6:text","title":"The Boy Who Held On to the Stone · 7 of 10","body":"And Shiva came out of the stone. Out of the very thing the boy was holding — because the boy had wrapped himself round it and there was nowhere for anybody to reach him that was not Shiva.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-markandeya.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.markandeya|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Held On to the Stone","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.markandeya-7":{"src":"story:pu.markandeya:scenes:7:text","title":"The Boy Who Held On to the Stone · 8 of 10","body":"\"Not this one,\" said Shiva. \"He is holding on to me.\" And Yama, who is not a villain and does not sulk, put down what he had brought and went away to the rest of his day.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-markandeya.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.markandeya|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Held On to the Stone","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-pu.markandeya-8":{"src":"story:pu.markandeya:scenes:8:text","title":"The Boy Who Held On to the Stone · 9 of 10","body":"Markandeya was given something rather odd: he stayed sixteen. Not for a while — for good. He is one of the chiranjivi, the long-living ones, and he turns up all over the Puranas afterwards, still sixteen, telling other people's stories.","art":"art/story/sm/pu-markandeya.jpg","route":"#/story/pu.markandeya|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Held On to the Stone","facts":[["From","Stories of the Gods"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bhekuli-biya-1":{"src":"story:fk.bhekuli-biya:scenes:1:text","title":"The Frogs' Wedding · 2 of 10","body":"And one year it did not. The clouds came to the edge of the hills and stopped there, day after day, and the fields cracked, and the grown-ups stopped joking at the tea stall and started standing about looking at the sky.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bhekuli-biya.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bhekuli-biya|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Frogs' Wedding","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bhekuli-biya-2":{"src":"story:fk.bhekuli-biya:scenes:2:text","title":"The Frogs' Wedding · 3 of 10","body":"\"Grandmother,\" said a girl called Junaki, \"the frogs have stopped.\" It was true. The one sound you get all summer in Assam — the whole pond going at once — had gone quiet.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bhekuli-biya.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bhekuli-biya|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Frogs' Wedding","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bhekuli-biya-3":{"src":"story:fk.bhekuli-biya:scenes:3:text","title":"The Frogs' Wedding · 4 of 10","body":"Her grandmother put down what she was doing. \"Then that is what we do,\" she said. \"It is what we have always done when the rain is late. But it is not a thing grown-ups can do. It has to be the children.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bhekuli-biya.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bhekuli-biya|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Frogs' Wedding","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bhekuli-biya-4":{"src":"story:fk.bhekuli-biya:scenes:4:text","title":"The Frogs' Wedding · 5 of 10","body":"And she explained it. Find two frogs. Marry them. Properly — with the songs, the turmeric, the feast, the little procession, the lot. Not as a joke.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bhekuli-biya.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bhekuli-biya|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Frogs' Wedding","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bhekuli-biya-6":{"src":"story:fk.bhekuli-biya:scenes:6:text","title":"The Frogs' Wedding · 7 of 10","body":"And then the girls sang the wedding songs, the real ones, all the way through — including the ones that are supposed to make the bride's family cry — and the whole village came out to watch, and a woman of eighty laughed until she had to sit down on a step.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bhekuli-biya.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bhekuli-biya|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Frogs' Wedding","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bhekuli-biya-7":{"src":"story:fk.bhekuli-biya:scenes:7:text","title":"The Frogs' Wedding · 8 of 10","body":"They let the two of them go into the pond together at the end. The frogs, who had had a long afternoon, went straight to the bottom and stayed there.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bhekuli-biya.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bhekuli-biya|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Frogs' Wedding","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bhekuli-biya-8":{"src":"story:fk.bhekuli-biya:scenes:8:text","title":"The Frogs' Wedding · 9 of 10","body":"It rained that night. Junaki woke up to it on the tin roof, which is the loudest and best sound in the world, and she lay there listening and did not go back to sleep for an hour.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bhekuli-biya.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bhekuli-biya|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Frogs' Wedding","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.lepcha-tower-1":{"src":"story:fk.lepcha-tower:scenes:1:text","title":"The Tower of Cooking Pots · 2 of 9","body":"But long ago, the story goes, there was a generation who got ambitious. They wanted to go up. Not up the mountain — past it. They wanted to reach the sky itself and see what was being kept there.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lepcha-tower.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lepcha-tower|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tower of Cooking Pots","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.lepcha-tower-2":{"src":"story:fk.lepcha-tower:scenes:2:text","title":"The Tower of Cooking Pots · 3 of 9","body":"\"Pots,\" said somebody. \"We make thousands of them. Stack them. One on another, and another on that.\" And it was such a simple idea that the whole people started making pots, and stacking, and it worked.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lepcha-tower.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lepcha-tower|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tower of Cooking Pots","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.lepcha-tower-3":{"src":"story:fk.lepcha-tower:scenes:3:text","title":"The Tower of Cooking Pots · 4 of 9","body":"The tower went up past the tree line. Past the snow line. There were people living on it, halfway up, passing pots hand to hand in a line that went out of sight into the cloud.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lepcha-tower.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lepcha-tower|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tower of Cooking Pots","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.lepcha-tower-5":{"src":"story:fk.lepcha-tower:scenes:5:text","title":"The Tower of Cooking Pots · 6 of 9","body":"He pulled the pot out. And the tower — the whole enormous impossible thing, with everybody's cousins on it — leaned, and let go, and came down the mountainside in a noise nobody there ever forgot.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lepcha-tower.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lepcha-tower|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tower of Cooking Pots","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.lepcha-tower-6":{"src":"story:fk.lepcha-tower:scenes:6:text","title":"The Tower of Cooking Pots · 7 of 9","body":"The survivors ran in every direction. And that, the Lepcha say, is why there are so many different languages in these hills — they scattered so far, and stayed apart so long, that they came out of it unable to understand one another.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lepcha-tower.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lepcha-tower|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tower of Cooking Pots","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.lepcha-tower-7":{"src":"story:fk.lepcha-tower:scenes:7:text","title":"The Tower of Cooking Pots · 8 of 9","body":"And it is why nobody in the old way of thinking goes to the very top of Kanchenjunga. Not because it cannot be done. Because it is not what you do to family.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lepcha-tower.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lepcha-tower|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tower of Cooking Pots","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.pebet-1":{"src":"story:fk.pebet:scenes:1:text","title":"Pebet and the Cat · 2 of 10","body":"A cat found the nest. He did not pounce. He sat down a little way off, made his voice very soft, and said: \"Sister. What beautiful children. Let me be their uncle. Let me carry one on my back and show him the world.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-pebet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.pebet|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Pebet and the Cat","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.pebet-2":{"src":"story:fk.pebet:scenes:2:text","title":"Pebet and the Cat · 3 of 10","body":"\"How kind,\" said Pebet, who was not fooled for one second. \"Come tomorrow.\" And the moment he was gone she got the whole family up and started teaching them, urgently, the only thing that was going to matter: how to fly.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-pebet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.pebet|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Pebet and the Cat","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.pebet-3":{"src":"story:fk.pebet:scenes:3:text","title":"Pebet and the Cat · 4 of 10","body":"They practised all that day and all the next. Five of them got it — wobbling, crash-landing, getting up and going again. The sixth was the smallest and could not do it yet, and cried about it, and was told he did not have to be sorry.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-pebet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.pebet|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Pebet and the Cat","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.pebet-4":{"src":"story:fk.pebet:scenes:4:text","title":"Pebet and the Cat · 5 of 10","body":"The cat came back. \"Uncle is here!\" he called up, sweet as anything. \"Who is coming for a ride?\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-pebet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.pebet|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Pebet and the Cat","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.pebet-6":{"src":"story:fk.pebet:scenes:6:text","title":"Pebet and the Cat · 7 of 10","body":"\"Up,\" said Pebet. \"All of you. Now.\" Five of them went, badly, in five directions. Then she got underneath the smallest one, took his weight, and went — low, slow, and heavy, over the cat's head and into the thorn bush.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-pebet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.pebet|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Pebet and the Cat","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.pebet-7":{"src":"story:fk.pebet:scenes:7:text","title":"Pebet and the Cat · 8 of 10","body":"The cat went up after them and got stuck in the thorns halfway, and had to back out one paw at a time, and came out with his ears down and his dignity in pieces.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-pebet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.pebet|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Pebet and the Cat","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.pebet-8":{"src":"story:fk.pebet:scenes:8:text","title":"Pebet and the Cat · 9 of 10","body":"And Pebet sat in the middle of the bush with six chicks around her, all of them talking at once about how well they had done, and did not correct a single one of them.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-pebet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.pebet|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Pebet and the Cat","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hojagiri-pots-1":{"src":"story:fk.hojagiri-pots:scenes:1:text","title":"The Girls Who Danced on the Water Pots · 2 of 9","body":"The old women say that Mailuma does not care much for loud thanks. \"Anyone can shout,\" a grandmother tells the girls. \"She watches for whether you can keep your balance when it matters.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hojagiri-pots.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hojagiri-pots|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girls Who Danced on the Water Pots","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hojagiri-pots-2":{"src":"story:fk.hojagiri-pots:scenes:2:text","title":"The Girls Who Danced on the Water Pots · 3 of 9","body":"Which is where the dance comes from, and it is not an easy one. A girl stands on an upturned earthen pitcher. On her head goes a bottle. On the bottle goes a lamp, lit. In her hands, a cloth and sometimes another lamp again.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hojagiri-pots.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hojagiri-pots|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girls Who Danced on the Water Pots","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hojagiri-pots-3":{"src":"story:fk.hojagiri-pots:scenes:3:text","title":"The Girls Who Danced on the Water Pots · 4 of 9","body":"And then, standing on the pitcher, she dances. Only the lower half of her body moves — knees, hips, feet on a curved clay surface the size of a dinner plate — while everything above the waist stays perfectly, impossibly still, because there is a flame up there.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hojagiri-pots.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hojagiri-pots|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girls Who Danced on the Water Pots","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hojagiri-pots-5":{"src":"story:fk.hojagiri-pots:scenes:5:text","title":"The Girls Who Danced on the Water Pots · 6 of 9","body":"\"You keep looking up at the flame,\" said her grandmother. \"Do not. The flame will look after itself if the pot is right. Feel the pot with your feet and forget everything above your shoulders.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hojagiri-pots.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hojagiri-pots|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girls Who Danced on the Water Pots","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hojagiri-pots-6":{"src":"story:fk.hojagiri-pots:scenes:6:text","title":"The Girls Who Danced on the Water Pots · 7 of 9","body":"On the night, the drums started and eight girls went up onto their pitchers at once, and the lamps went on their heads, and the whole village stopped talking. Kwrwi shut her eyes for the first eight beats and felt for the curve of the clay with the balls of her feet.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hojagiri-pots.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hojagiri-pots|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girls Who Danced on the Water Pots","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hojagiri-pots-7":{"src":"story:fk.hojagiri-pots:scenes:7:text","title":"The Girls Who Danced on the Water Pots · 8 of 9","body":"She danced the whole thing. So did all eight of them. Nothing came down, nothing went out, and the lamps moved through the dark in eight steady lines while the drums got faster and faster underneath.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hojagiri-pots.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hojagiri-pots|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girls Who Danced on the Water Pots","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.parashuram-coast-1":{"src":"story:fk.parashuram-coast:scenes:1:text","title":"The Arrow That Made the Coast · 2 of 9","body":"Parashurama had finished with fighting. He had put it all down and he wanted somewhere quiet to live and to settle the people who had come with him. He walked west until he could not walk any further, and stood on the top of the Ghats with the sea directly below him.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-parashuram-coast.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.parashuram-coast|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Arrow That Made the Coast","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.parashuram-coast-2":{"src":"story:fk.parashuram-coast:scenes:2:text","title":"The Arrow That Made the Coast · 3 of 9","body":"\"There is no land,\" said the people behind him. \"There is mountain, and then there is water, and there is nothing in between.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-parashuram-coast.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.parashuram-coast|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Arrow That Made the Coast","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.parashuram-coast-3":{"src":"story:fk.parashuram-coast:scenes:3:text","title":"The Arrow That Made the Coast · 4 of 9","body":"He looked at the sea for a long time. Then he took his bow off his shoulder.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-parashuram-coast.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.parashuram-coast|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Arrow That Made the Coast","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.parashuram-coast-5":{"src":"story:fk.parashuram-coast:scenes:5:text","title":"The Arrow That Made the Coast · 6 of 9","body":"And the sea, in the story, went back. Not in a wave — quietly, the way a tide goes out and does not come in again. And where it had been there was ground: dark, wet, salty ground, from the foot of the mountains all the way out to where the arrow had fallen.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-parashuram-coast.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.parashuram-coast|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Arrow That Made the Coast","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.parashuram-coast-6":{"src":"story:fk.parashuram-coast:scenes:6:text","title":"The Arrow That Made the Coast · 7 of 9","body":"It was no good at first. Nothing will grow in soil the sea has just left. So they brought in earth, and they planted the things that will put up with salt — the coconut, the cashew, the mango — and worked it season after season until it turned into the greenest place any of them had ever seen.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-parashuram-coast.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.parashuram-coast|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Arrow That Made the Coast","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.parashuram-coast-7":{"src":"story:fk.parashuram-coast:scenes:7:text","title":"The Arrow That Made the Coast · 8 of 9","body":"The Konkan coast runs from Gujarat down through Goa to Kerala, and versions of this story are told the whole length of it. Every stretch of it says the arrow came down just off their own beach.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-parashuram-coast.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.parashuram-coast|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Arrow That Made the Coast","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hadimba-cedar-1":{"src":"story:fk.hadimba-cedar:scenes:1:text","title":"The Grandmother in the Cedar Wood · 2 of 9","body":"The Mahabharata tells the first part. The Pandavas were in exile, sleeping in a forest, and the forest belonged to Hidimba and her brother — forest people, powerful, not part of anybody's kingdom, and thoroughly feared by everyone who was.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hadimba-cedar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hadimba-cedar|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Grandmother in the Cedar Wood","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hadimba-cedar-2":{"src":"story:fk.hadimba-cedar:scenes:2:text","title":"The Grandmother in the Cedar Wood · 3 of 9","body":"Her brother wanted the strangers gone. Hidimba went to look at them and came back and said no. \"The big one,\" she said. \"The one who carries the others when they are tired. I am not doing anything to him.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hadimba-cedar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hadimba-cedar|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Grandmother in the Cedar Wood","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hadimba-cedar-3":{"src":"story:fk.hadimba-cedar:scenes:3:text","title":"The Grandmother in the Cedar Wood · 4 of 9","body":"There was a fight about it, the way there is. And when it was over, Hidimba walked up to Bhima, who was the strongest man alive, and said what she wanted — plainly, in front of his mother, in front of everybody.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hadimba-cedar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hadimba-cedar|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Grandmother in the Cedar Wood","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hadimba-cedar-5":{"src":"story:fk.hadimba-cedar:scenes:5:text","title":"The Grandmother in the Cedar Wood · 6 of 9","body":"Their son was Ghatotkacha, who grew up in his mother's forest and became one of the great figures of the epic in his own right. And Bhima went back to his brothers when it was time, as agreed, and Hidimba stayed in the hills.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hadimba-cedar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hadimba-cedar|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Grandmother in the Cedar Wood","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hadimba-cedar-6":{"src":"story:fk.hadimba-cedar:scenes:6:text","title":"The Grandmother in the Cedar Wood · 7 of 9","body":"And here is the part that belongs to Himachal rather than to the epic. She did not disappear out of the story. In the Kullu valley she stayed — and became the goddess of the place. Hadimba Devi. Not a side character. The one whose permission you need.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hadimba-cedar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hadimba-cedar|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Grandmother in the Cedar Wood","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hadimba-cedar-7":{"src":"story:fk.hadimba-cedar:scenes:7:text","title":"The Grandmother in the Cedar Wood · 8 of 9","body":"At the Kullu Dussehra the village deities of the whole valley come down in palanquins, carried on shoulders for miles along the mountain roads, and the festival waits for her. It does not begin until Hadimba arrives.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hadimba-cedar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hadimba-cedar|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Grandmother in the Cedar Wood","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.santhal-first-birds-1":{"src":"story:fk.santhal-first-birds:scenes:1:text","title":"The Two Birds and the First Morning · 2 of 9","body":"Over that water flew two birds, made by Thakur Jiu, the great one, and they were extremely tired, because there is nowhere to land on an ocean. They flew, and flew, and got no rest.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-santhal-first-birds.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.santhal-first-birds|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Birds and the First Morning","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.santhal-first-birds-2":{"src":"story:fk.santhal-first-birds:scenes:2:text","title":"The Two Birds and the First Morning · 3 of 9","body":"\"We cannot go on,\" said the birds. \"Give us somewhere to put our feet.\" And so the making of the earth began — and it began with asking the creatures who were already in the water to help.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-santhal-first-birds.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.santhal-first-birds|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Birds and the First Morning","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.santhal-first-birds-3":{"src":"story:fk.santhal-first-birds:scenes:3:text","title":"The Two Birds and the First Morning · 4 of 9","body":"They asked the crab. They asked the crocodile. They asked the prawn. Each one went down and each one came back with nothing, because they brought the soil up in their claws and the water washed it away on the way up.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-santhal-first-birds.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.santhal-first-birds|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Birds and the First Morning","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.santhal-first-birds-5":{"src":"story:fk.santhal-first-birds:scenes:5:text","title":"The Two Birds and the First Morning · 6 of 9","body":"The tortoise was anchored at the four corners and held still. The earthworm went down, swallowed the soil, came up, and let it out on the tortoise's back — where the water could not wash it away. Again. And again. And the earth grew.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-santhal-first-birds.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.santhal-first-birds|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Birds and the First Morning","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.santhal-first-birds-6":{"src":"story:fk.santhal-first-birds:scenes:6:text","title":"The Two Birds and the First Morning · 7 of 9","body":"The birds came down on it at last and slept. And on that new ground they made a nest, and there were two eggs in it, and out of those eggs came Pilchu Haram and Pilchu Budhi — the first man and the first woman, the grandfather and the grandmother of the Santhals.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-santhal-first-birds.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.santhal-first-birds|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Birds and the First Morning","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.santhal-first-birds-7":{"src":"story:fk.santhal-first-birds:scenes:7:text","title":"The Two Birds and the First Morning · 8 of 9","body":"They grew up on the new earth. They learned what could be eaten and what could not. They had children, and the children went off in different directions, and the different directions became the twelve clans, and Santhal families still know which one they belong to.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-santhal-first-birds.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.santhal-first-birds|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Birds and the First Morning","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.karaikal-mango-1":{"src":"story:fk.karaikal-mango:scenes:1:text","title":"The Second Mango · 2 of 10","body":"One morning her husband Paramadattan sent home two mangoes from the market — good ones, the sort you save — with a message that he would eat them at lunch.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karaikal-mango.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karaikal-mango|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Second Mango","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.karaikal-mango-2":{"src":"story:fk.karaikal-mango:scenes:2:text","title":"The Second Mango · 3 of 10","body":"And before lunch a wandering ascetic came to the door, thin and dusty and swaying slightly, who had not eaten in longer than he could remember. She had rice ready but no curry to go with it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karaikal-mango.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karaikal-mango|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Second Mango","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.karaikal-mango-3":{"src":"story:fk.karaikal-mango:scenes:3:text","title":"The Second Mango · 4 of 10","body":"She gave him one of the mangoes with the rice, and he ate, and blessed the house, and went off up the road. And she went back to the kitchen thinking: I will explain it this evening, and he will laugh.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karaikal-mango.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karaikal-mango|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Second Mango","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.karaikal-mango-4":{"src":"story:fk.karaikal-mango:scenes:4:text","title":"The Second Mango · 5 of 10","body":"Her husband came home early. He ate the first mango and said it was the best he had had all year. \"Bring the other one.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karaikal-mango.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karaikal-mango|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Second Mango","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.karaikal-mango-6":{"src":"story:fk.karaikal-mango:scenes:6:text","title":"The Second Mango · 7 of 10","body":"And there was a mango in her hand. She carried it in and gave it to him, and he took one bite and stopped chewing, because it did not taste like a mango from any market anywhere.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karaikal-mango.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karaikal-mango|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Second Mango","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.karaikal-mango-7":{"src":"story:fk.karaikal-mango:scenes:7:text","title":"The Second Mango · 8 of 10","body":"\"Where did this come from?\" She told him. All of it, plainly — the ascetic, the first mango, the shut eyes, the second one. And Paramadattan put the fruit down and looked at his wife as though he had never seen her before, and was, quite suddenly, frightened of her.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karaikal-mango.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karaikal-mango|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Second Mango","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.karaikal-mango-8":{"src":"story:fk.karaikal-mango:scenes:8:text","title":"The Second Mango · 9 of 10","body":"He never really got past it. And Punitavati, who had been given something she had not asked for, eventually stopped trying to live an ordinary life around it. She went, and she sang, and the songs she made are still sung in Tamil today.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-karaikal-mango.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.karaikal-mango|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Second Mango","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.andaman-fire-1":{"src":"story:fk.andaman-fire:scenes:1:text","title":"The Bird Who Carried the Fire · 2 of 9","body":"In the beginning, they say, people had no fire. They ate everything raw and were cold at night and could not see past the edge of the trees when the dark came down.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-andaman-fire.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.andaman-fire|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bird Who Carried the Fire","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.andaman-fire-2":{"src":"story:fk.andaman-fire:scenes:2:text","title":"The Bird Who Carried the Fire · 3 of 9","body":"There was fire in the world. Exactly one. It belonged to Biliku, who is the north-east wind and the storms that come with her, and she kept it, and she was not the sort of person you asked twice.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-andaman-fire.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.andaman-fire|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bird Who Carried the Fire","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.andaman-fire-3":{"src":"story:fk.andaman-fire:scenes:3:text","title":"The Bird Who Carried the Fire · 4 of 9","body":"The people talked about it for a long time. Someone would have to go. It could not be anybody big — anybody big would be seen coming a mile off.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-andaman-fire.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.andaman-fire|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bird Who Carried the Fire","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.andaman-fire-5":{"src":"story:fk.andaman-fire:scenes:5:text","title":"The Bird Who Carried the Fire · 6 of 9","body":"He did not get away with it. She woke, and she came after him the way a storm comes after anything, and the whole sky went over in one go — but he was small and fast and low over the water, and he kept hold of the fire the whole way.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-andaman-fire.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.andaman-fire|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bird Who Carried the Fire","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.andaman-fire-6":{"src":"story:fk.andaman-fire:scenes:6:text","title":"The Bird Who Carried the Fire · 7 of 9","body":"He dropped it in the middle of the camp. And people took a piece each, and carried it home, and after that there was fire on the islands and it never went out — because from then on somebody was always keeping it going.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-andaman-fire.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.andaman-fire|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bird Who Carried the Fire","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.andaman-fire-7":{"src":"story:fk.andaman-fire:scenes:7:text","title":"The Bird Who Carried the Fire · 8 of 9","body":"Biliku stayed angry, and in the tellings she is still angry, and the great storms that come across those islands from the north-east are her. You do not treat her lightly. But the fire stayed.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-andaman-fire.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.andaman-fire|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Bird Who Carried the Fire","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-rice-1":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-rice:scenes:1:text","title":"Abo Tani and the First Handful · 2 of 9","body":"In the beginning Abo Tani lived the way you live when nobody has worked anything out yet. He ate what he found. Some days he found a lot and some days he found nothing, and there was no way of telling which sort of day it was going to be.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-rice.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-rice|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Abo Tani and the First Handful","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-rice-2":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-rice:scenes:2:text","title":"Abo Tani and the First Handful · 3 of 9","body":"The stories say the spirits — the wiyus — had things he did not: the seed, and the knowing what to do with it. And they were not always friendly, and Abo Tani had to be quick, and the tellings of exactly how he got the seed differ from one valley to the next.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-rice.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-rice|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Abo Tani and the First Handful","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-rice-3":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-rice:scenes:3:text","title":"Abo Tani and the First Handful · 4 of 9","body":"But he got it. He cut the slope, he burned it clean, he put the seed in and waited, and one morning the whole hillside came up green in lines. And in the autumn there was rice — more than he could eat, more than he could carry, the first harvest there had ever been.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-rice.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-rice|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Abo Tani and the First Handful","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-rice-5":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-rice:scenes:5:text","title":"Abo Tani and the First Handful · 6 of 9","body":"\"This did not come from me,\" said Abo Tani. \"The slope was here. The rain was here. The seed came from somewhere else. I only did the digging.\" And he took the first handful and put it out — for the ground, for the water, for whoever had gone hungry that year.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-rice.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-rice|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Abo Tani and the First Handful","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-rice-6":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-rice:scenes:6:text","title":"Abo Tani and the First Handful · 7 of 9","body":"And after that the year had a shape to it. You clear, you sow, you wait, you harvest, and before any of it goes in your mouth some of it goes out of the house. His children did it. Their children did it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-rice.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-rice|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Abo Tani and the First Handful","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-rice-7":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-rice:scenes:7:text","title":"Abo Tani and the First Handful · 8 of 9","body":"The mithun came later — the great half-wild cattle of these hills, which are not milked and are not worked, and which a family keeps as the thing you bring out when something matters. But the rule was already set by then, and the mithun follows it: it is for giving.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-rice.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-rice|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Abo Tani and the First Handful","facts":[["From","Corners Not Yet Lit"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.panthirukulam-1":{"src":"story:fk.panthirukulam:scenes:1:text","title":"Twelve Houses, One Mother · 2 of 10","body":"By a river one day he met a young woman who answered, without even putting down the washing, a riddle that had beaten every scholar he knew. He came back the next day with a harder one. She answered that too, and added a better one of her own. He married her, which was the first fully sensible thing he had ever done.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Twelve Houses, One Mother","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.panthirukulam-2":{"src":"story:fk.panthirukulam:scenes:2:text","title":"Twelve Houses, One Mother · 3 of 10","body":"They took to the road together, the way wandering scholars did, and over the years twelve children were born to them — each one in a different place along the way.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Twelve Houses, One Mother","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.panthirukulam-3":{"src":"story:fk.panthirukulam:scenes:3:text","title":"Twelve Houses, One Mother · 4 of 10","body":"And here the story does a hard thing, and it knows it is hard. Each time a baby came, Vararuchi asked his wife one question: \"Does the child have a mouth?\" \"Of course,\" she said. \"Then the One who gave the mouth will send the food,\" he said. \"Lay the child down gently, and walk on.\" And, trusting something bigger than her own breaking heart, she did.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Twelve Houses, One Mother","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.panthirukulam-4":{"src":"story:fk.panthirukulam:scenes:4:text","title":"Twelve Houses, One Mother · 5 of 10","body":"A baby, lying wrapped by the side of a path in Kerala, where somebody passes every few minutes. The story knows exactly what it is doing.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Twelve Houses, One Mother","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.panthirukulam-6":{"src":"story:fk.panthirukulam:scenes:6:text","title":"Twelve Houses, One Mother · 7 of 10","body":"One, called Uppukottan, was raised in a salt trader's house where the family prayed as Muslims pray, and he grew up in that faith and carried salt down every road in the country. One never settled in any house at all — you will meet him in the next story, pushing a boulder up a hill. Twelve homes, twelve trades, more than one way of praying. One mother.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Twelve Houses, One Mother","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.panthirukulam-7":{"src":"story:fk.panthirukulam:scenes:7:text","title":"Twelve Houses, One Mother · 8 of 10","body":"And once a year, the story says, all twelve came together under one roof for their mother's feast day — the drummer and the fire keeper, the carpenter and the salt trader, each bringing what his own house made, each eating in the way of his own home, at the same time, in the same courtyard.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Twelve Houses, One Mother","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.panthirukulam-8":{"src":"story:fk.panthirukulam:scenes:8:text","title":"Twelve Houses, One Mother · 9 of 10","body":"The neighbours were scandalised, of course. Such different people, at one feast! And the story just looks at the neighbours, and waits, until they work it out: same mother.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-panthirukulam.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.panthirukulam|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Twelve Houses, One Mother","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.naranath-boulder-1":{"src":"story:fk.naranath-boulder:scenes:1:text","title":"The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up · 2 of 10","body":"His days went like this. At dawn he put his shoulder to an enormous boulder at the bottom of the hill at Rayiranellur, and he pushed. All morning. All afternoon. Grunting, sweating, inch by inch, all the way up.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.naranath-boulder-2":{"src":"story:fk.naranath-boulder:scenes:2:text","title":"The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up · 3 of 10","body":"And at the top, in the last light, he stood back, dusted his hands — and let it go. The boulder went thundering and bouncing all the way back down to exactly where it had started. And Naranath threw back his head and laughed until he had to sit down.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.naranath-boulder-3":{"src":"story:fk.naranath-boulder:scenes:3:text","title":"The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up · 4 of 10","body":"\"Mad,\" said the villagers, comfortably, and went back to their dinners. But the children were braver than the grown-ups, the way children are, and one evening a few of them climbed up and asked him straight out: \"Why do you laugh when your whole day's work rolls away?\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.naranath-boulder-4":{"src":"story:fk.naranath-boulder:scenes:4:text","title":"The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up · 5 of 10","body":"\"Everyone in the valley pushes something uphill all day,\" he told the children, wiping his eyes. \"Money up the hill, and it rolls down. Fame up the hill, and it rolls down. They cry about it every evening. I am the only one who knew it would roll — so I am the only one laughing.\" And he patted the boulder like an old bullock.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.naranath-boulder-6":{"src":"story:fk.naranath-boulder:scenes:6:text","title":"The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up · 7 of 10","body":"\"Ask me for something,\" said the goddess, who rather liked him. Naranath thought about it. He asked when his life would end, and she told him, because he asked so calmly. \"Could I have one day more?\" No, she said — that is not given. \"One day less, then?\" Not that either. \"Then, Amma,\" said Naranath, \"the boons I actually need, nobody can give.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.naranath-boulder-7":{"src":"story:fk.naranath-boulder:scenes:7:text","title":"The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up · 8 of 10","body":"The goddess insisted — a boon is a boon. So Naranath looked down at his legs. One had been swollen since he was young, and ached. \"Then move the swelling,\" he said, \"from my left leg to my right. For a change.\" And she did. And Naranath laughed harder than he had ever laughed at the boulder — and the goddess, the story swears, laughed with him.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.naranath-boulder-8":{"src":"story:fk.naranath-boulder:scenes:8:text","title":"The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up · 9 of 10","body":"He walked on through the land all his life, wanting nothing, owing nothing, laughing at the right moments — and the ones who called him mad slowly noticed that he was the only person in Kerala nobody could make unhappy.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naranath-boulder.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naranath-boulder|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Man Who Rolled the Boulder Up","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kochunni-1":{"src":"story:fk.kochunni:scenes:1:text","title":"The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up · 2 of 9","body":"What he watched most was the kalari — the training yard where boys learned kalaripayattu, the old exercise art of Kerala. It was not for boys like him; nobody would pay his way in. So he watched through the fence, every evening, and practised what he saw, alone, by moonlight, until the moves lived in his body.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kochunni.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kochunni|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kochunni-2":{"src":"story:fk.kochunni:scenes:2:text","title":"The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up · 3 of 9","body":"The teacher caught him at it one night. Stood in the shadows and watched a shop boy run through the whole evening's lesson, alone, correctly. \"Come inside,\" said the teacher at last. \"A boy who teaches himself through a fence will not be kept out by me.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kochunni.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kochunni|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kochunni-3":{"src":"story:fk.kochunni:scenes:3:text","title":"The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up · 4 of 9","body":"Kochunni grew up strong and quick, able to climb a wall like a cat and cross a courtyard without a sound. And here the story stops smiling for a moment, because of what he did with all that. He took things. From the locked storerooms of men who had grown rich squeezing the hungry — but he took, and taking is taking, and this story is not going to pretend otherwise.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kochunni.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kochunni|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kochunni-5":{"src":"story:fk.kochunni:scenes:5:text","title":"The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up · 6 of 9","body":"And his word was iron. If Kochunni said a thing would happen, it happened; if he promised a man safety, that man was safe. Even the merchants he had robbed admitted it through their teeth: the thief was the fairest man in the district.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kochunni.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kochunni|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kochunni-6":{"src":"story:fk.kochunni:scenes:6:text","title":"The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up · 7 of 9","body":"So when the soldiers came to Kayamkulam to search for him, house by house, they found something they had not planned for. Silence. In every lane, in every doorway, from every fisherman and washerwoman and rice farmer: nobody had seen him. Nobody knew anything at all.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kochunni.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kochunni|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kochunni-7":{"src":"story:fk.kochunni:scenes:7:text","title":"The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up · 8 of 9","body":"The law caught up with him in the end, because it does, and his last years were spent locked away. And even then — this is the part the old tellings linger on — people carried food to him, and told his stories out loud, and went on leaving his name out of their answers.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kochunni.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kochunni|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Man the Poor Would Not Give Up","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kathanar-1":{"src":"story:fk.kathanar:scenes:1:text","title":"The Priest Who Talked to the Dark · 2 of 10","body":"He began as a boy called Poulose who looked after the church's cattle, and one day, following a strayed cow, he went too far into the high forest and was lost. The people who found him were hill people who lived far from any town, and who knew things about the forest and the night that the valley folk had long forgotten. They took him in for years, and taught him.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kathanar-2":{"src":"story:fk.kathanar:scenes:2:text","title":"The Priest Who Talked to the Dark · 3 of 10","body":"When he came home at last, he served his church and in time became its priest — its kathanar, which is simply the old Kerala word for a priest. Kadamattathu Kathanar: the priest of Kadamattom. And word slowly went round that this particular priest was not afraid of the dark. At all.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kathanar-3":{"src":"story:fk.kathanar:scenes:3:text","title":"The Priest Who Talked to the Dark · 4 of 10","body":"Because here is what he had learned in the hills, and it is worth learning: the dark is mostly full of lonely things, not wicked ones.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kathanar-4":{"src":"story:fk.kathanar:scenes:4:text","title":"The Priest Who Talked to the Dark · 5 of 10","body":"A family came to him once, worn out and grey-faced. Something was in their house. Pots shifted by themselves. The roof rattled on windless nights. Nobody had slept in weeks. The Kathanar picked up his lamp and walked home with them, and sat down in the middle of the noisiest room, and waited for full dark.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kathanar-6":{"src":"story:fk.kathanar:scenes:6:text","title":"The Priest Who Talked to the Dark · 7 of 10","body":"So he gave it what it wanted. A place of its own — a great old tree at the edge of the land — and, even better, a job: keeping watch. \"Spirits are like children at a wedding,\" the Kathanar used to say. \"Dreadful when ignored. Magnificent when given something to do.\" The pots never moved again, and the family slept.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kathanar-7":{"src":"story:fk.kathanar:scenes:7:text","title":"The Priest Who Talked to the Dark · 8 of 10","body":"One story tells of a spirit who followed him for miles along a night road, planning mischief the whole way — and who arrived at Kadamattom, somehow, as his friend, and stayed. That was the pattern with him. Whatever set out to frighten the Kathanar ended up working for him.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kathanar-8":{"src":"story:fk.kathanar:scenes:8:text","title":"The Priest Who Talked to the Dark · 9 of 10","body":"And people of every faith sent for him — Hindu households, Muslim households, Christian households — because a lamp in the dark does not check the name on the door. He went to all of them alike, in his priest's robes, with his lamp.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kathanar.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kathanar|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Priest Who Talked to the Dark","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.theyyam-mirror-1":{"src":"story:fk.theyyam-mirror:scenes:1:text","title":"The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess · 2 of 10","body":"A girl called Devu sat up one night to watch it happen to her own grandfather. He sat perfectly still on a mat in the temple yard while artists worked on him by lamplight, hour after hour — orange and red and black, fine lines drawn on his face with a slip of coconut leaf, a headdress being built nearby that stood taller than a door.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.theyyam-mirror-2":{"src":"story:fk.theyyam-mirror:scenes:2:text","title":"The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess · 3 of 10","body":"Her family had carried this theyyam for generations — the painting, the songs, the steps, handed down like land. And here is the part to sit with. The families who carry the gods in Malabar are, most of them, families whom others too often put last — for the work they did, and the houses they were born into. On theyyam night, that turns upside down.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.theyyam-mirror-3":{"src":"story:fk.theyyam-mirror:scenes:3:text","title":"The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess · 4 of 10","body":"Because when the drums began before dawn, everyone came, and everyone stood, and everyone waited on her grandfather. The landowner whose fields he worked. The shopkeeper. The teacher. On this night, in this yard, the man they walked past all year was the one they had all come to bow to.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.theyyam-mirror-4":{"src":"story:fk.theyyam-mirror:scenes:4:text","title":"The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess · 5 of 10","body":"The headdress was lifted onto him. The drums climbed. And then came the moment the whole night had been building to: they held up a small mirror in front of his painted face. He looked into it. And the tellings all agree — the person who looks back out of that mirror is not the performer anymore. Devu's grandfather looked into the glass, and the goddess looked out.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.theyyam-mirror-6":{"src":"story:fk.theyyam-mirror:scenes:6:text","title":"The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess · 7 of 10","body":"The goddess blessed each person by name — she knew them all. She scolded gently where scolding was needed. She settled the quarrel between the two houses that had not spoken since the wedding. And the drums held it all up like a floor, till the sky went grey.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.theyyam-mirror-7":{"src":"story:fk.theyyam-mirror:scenes:7:text","title":"The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess · 8 of 10","body":"At dawn the headdress was lifted off, and the paint was wiped away, and there on the mat sat Devu's grandfather — small again, tired to the bone, drinking hot tea with both hands wobbling. Devu crept up and asked him: \"Where did the goddess go?\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.theyyam-mirror-8":{"src":"story:fk.theyyam-mirror:scenes:8:text","title":"The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess · 9 of 10","body":"\"Home,\" said her grandfather, looking out at the morning. \"Until next year.\" He took another sip of tea, and smiled at her. \"Don't worry. Now she knows the way.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-theyyam-mirror.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.theyyam-mirror|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Night Grandfather Became a Goddess","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.aranmula-boat-1":{"src":"story:fk.aranmula-boat:scenes:1:text","title":"The Race That Nobody Must Win · 2 of 9","body":"Long ago, the legend says, one household upriver was given a great honour: every year at Onam time, their boat would carry the provisions for the temple feast down the Pampa — rice and coconuts, plantains and jaggery, everything needed to feed every single person who came, with nobody turned away.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-aranmula-boat.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.aranmula-boat|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Race That Nobody Must Win","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.aranmula-boat-2":{"src":"story:fk.aranmula-boat:scenes:2:text","title":"The Race That Nobody Must Win · 3 of 9","body":"And one year, on a lonely dark stretch of the river, boats slid out from the shadows of the banks. Men who wanted the feast for themselves. The provision boat was slow and heavy and alone, and the river was long, and the night was very dark.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-aranmula-boat.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.aranmula-boat|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Race That Nobody Must Win","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.aranmula-boat-3":{"src":"story:fk.aranmula-boat:scenes:3:text","title":"The Race That Nobody Must Win · 4 of 9","body":"But a river bank is never as empty as it looks. Word ran along the water faster than any boat — a shout, a lamp, a conch — and village after village pushed out what it had. And what those villages had were chundan vallams: snake boats. Long as ten houses, curled high at the stern like a cobra's raised hood, a hundred rowers to a boat.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-aranmula-boat.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.aranmula-boat|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Race That Nobody Must Win","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.aranmula-boat-5":{"src":"story:fk.aranmula-boat:scenes:5:text","title":"The Race That Nobody Must Win · 6 of 9","body":"The feast arrived. The temple fed everyone, with nobody turned away. And the story smiles and says that Parthasarathy — who spent the great war not fighting but steering, right beside one man who needed him — was pleased that year, and not mainly by the food.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-aranmula-boat.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.aranmula-boat|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Race That Nobody Must Win","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.aranmula-boat-6":{"src":"story:fk.aranmula-boat:scenes:6:text","title":"The Race That Nobody Must Win · 7 of 9","body":"So it became the rule, and then the festival. Every year in the Onam season, the snake boats of the Pampa villages come back and go down the river with the feast boat — oars flashing in time, the boat songs called vanchipattu keeping the stroke, thousands singing on the banks.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-aranmula-boat.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.aranmula-boat|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Race That Nobody Must Win","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.aranmula-boat-7":{"src":"story:fk.aranmula-boat:scenes:7:text","title":"The Race That Nobody Must Win · 8 of 9","body":"And at Aranmula, the elders will tell you, it is not truly a race at all, whatever it looks like. The boats arrive together, because they are doing what their great-great-grandfathers' boats did: escorting the feast, at the pace of the one who needs them. The day is only ruined if somebody arrives alone.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-aranmula-boat.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.aranmula-boat|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Race That Nobody Must Win","facts":[["From","The Deep South"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-it.konark-chariot-1":{"src":"story:it.konark-chariot:scenes:1:text","title":"The Chariot That Cannot Move and Never Stops · 2 of 9","body":"It was built in the thirteenth century, by order of King Narasimhadeva the First of the Eastern Ganga dynasty, who ruled this coast. His builders were given an idea of pure poetry: the temple would not merely honour Surya, the sun. It would BE his chariot — the great chariot in which, as the katha tells it, the sun rides across the sky each day.","art":"art/story/sm/it-konark-chariot.jpg","route":"#/story/it.konark-chariot|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Chariot That Cannot Move and Never Stops","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-it.konark-chariot-2":{"src":"story:it.konark-chariot:scenes:2:text","title":"The Chariot That Cannot Move and Never Stops · 3 of 9","body":"So they built a chariot of stone the size of a hill. Along its sides they carved twelve pairs of gigantic wheels, taller than a grown man, every spoke and hub worked with carvings fine as jewellery. And at the front, straining forward in stone, seven mighty horses to draw the whole temple towards the dawn.","art":"art/story/sm/it-konark-chariot.jpg","route":"#/story/it.konark-chariot|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Chariot That Cannot Move and Never Stops","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-it.konark-chariot-3":{"src":"story:it.konark-chariot:scenes:3:text","title":"The Chariot That Cannot Move and Never Stops · 4 of 9","body":"Guides at Konark love to ask visitors the counting question, so here it is for you. Twelve pairs of wheels. Seven horses. The builders chose those numbers on purpose.","art":"art/story/sm/it-konark-chariot.jpg","route":"#/story/it.konark-chariot|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Chariot That Cannot Move and Never Stops","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-it.konark-chariot-5":{"src":"story:it.konark-chariot:scenes:5:text","title":"The Chariot That Cannot Move and Never Stops · 6 of 9","body":"Honesty about the evidence: the temple you see today is not complete. Its main tower — which would have been one of the tallest in India — fell in ruin centuries ago, and scholars still debate exactly when and why: the ground, the sea air, damage, neglect; the records are thin and historians say so rather than guessing.","art":"art/story/sm/it-konark-chariot.jpg","route":"#/story/it.konark-chariot|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Chariot That Cannot Move and Never Stops","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-it.konark-chariot-6":{"src":"story:it.konark-chariot:scenes:6:text","title":"The Chariot That Cannot Move and Never Stops · 7 of 9","body":"Look closely at the carvings and the temple stops being solemn: musicians, dancers, elephants, geese, wrestlers, families, whole crowded inches of thirteenth-century life. The builders carved their own world onto the sun's chariot — so in a way, everyone got to ride.","art":"art/story/sm/it-konark-chariot.jpg","route":"#/story/it.konark-chariot|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Chariot That Cannot Move and Never Stops","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-it.konark-chariot-7":{"src":"story:it.konark-chariot:scenes:7:text","title":"The Chariot That Cannot Move and Never Stops · 8 of 9","body":"In 1984, Konark was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site — one of the treasures the whole world has agreed to keep — and the Archaeological Survey of India tends it. Conservators work on it constantly; keeping a stone chariot is slow, careful, unending work, like keeping anything worth keeping.","art":"art/story/sm/it-konark-chariot.jpg","route":"#/story/it.konark-chariot|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Chariot That Cannot Move and Never Stops","facts":[["From","East & the Islands"],["Read","5 min"]]},"en-ramayana-4-1":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|4","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":4},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:3:cards:1:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Bow Nobody Could Lift","body":"He also had a daughter. She had been found in a furrow in a ploughed field when he was turning the first sod of the season, and he had picked her up out of the earth and taken her home, and that is why she is called Sita, and also Janaki, and also, in the south, Maithili.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 4","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","4 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-4-2":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|4","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":4},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:3:cards:2:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Bow Nobody Could Lift","body":"\"She was six,\" Janaka told the sage over dinner, sounding tired. \"The bow was in the way of her ball. She lifted the end of it with one hand and moved it. I had eleven ministers watching. So now I have told the world that whoever can string it may marry her, because what else was I supposed to do?\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 4","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","4 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-4-3":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|4","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":4},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:3:cards:3:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Bow Nobody Could Lift","body":"Kings had been coming for years. Big ones, clever ones, ones who brought their own engineers. They heaved and strained and had ropes brought and went home in a bad temper. One had brought an elephant. The bow had not so much as rolled.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 4","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","4 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-4-4":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|4","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":4},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:3:cards:4:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Bow Nobody Could Lift","body":"In many tellings — Kamban's Tamil one, and Tulsidas's — Sita and Rama see each other before any of this. She is in the palace garden picking flowers, he comes past the wall, and they look at each other for about as long as it takes to breathe out. Neither of them says anything. Neither of them forgets it either.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 4","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","4 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-4-6":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|4","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":4},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:3:cards:6:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Bow Nobody Could Lift","body":"Rama walked over. He did not roll up his sleeves or address the crowd. He walked round the cart once, the way you walk round a horse you are about to ride, and then he bent down and picked the bow up in the middle.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 4","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","4 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-4-7":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|4","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":4},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:3:cards:7:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Bow Nobody Could Lift","body":"The courtyard went completely silent. He set the end of it against his foot, pulled it into a curve, and reached for the string — and the bow, which was old, and tired, and had been waiting a great many years for exactly this, broke in half with a crack they heard in the next kingdom.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 4","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","4 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-4-8":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|4","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":4},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:3:cards:8:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Bow Nobody Could Lift","body":"People said afterwards that they had thought it was thunder. Birds came off every roof in Mithila at once. And Sita walked out into the courtyard with a garland in both hands, past a hundred kings, and did not hurry.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 4","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","4 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-4-9":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|4","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":4},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:3:cards:9:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Bow Nobody Could Lift","body":"It became four weddings instead of one, which is how these things go. Rama and Sita. Lakshmana and Sita's sister Urmila. Bharata and Mandavi. Shatrughna and Shrutakirti. Dasharatha came up from Ayodhya and cried at all four of them.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 4","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","4 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-7-1":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|7","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":7},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:6:cards:1:text","title":"The Ramayana · I Am Coming With You","body":"\"Do you know what my father used to say?\" said Sita, still folding. \"That a woman's place is not a room, it is beside the person she chose. I did not marry a palace. If you had been crowned tomorrow I would have sat next to you on the seat, and everybody would have called that correct. So I will walk next to you in the forest, and it is exactly as correct, and I am not discussing it.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 7","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","7 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-7-2":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|7","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":7},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:6:cards:2:text","title":"The Ramayana · I Am Coming With You","body":"\"There are wild animals — \" \"There are wild animals here, they simply wear better clothes.\" \"There is nothing to eat but roots.\" \"Then I will eat roots.\" \"Sita, please.\" \"Rama,\" she said, \"you are going to be gone fourteen years. Say the true reason or stop talking.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 7","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","7 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-7-3":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|7","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":7},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:6:cards:3:text","title":"The Ramayana · I Am Coming With You","body":"He did not have a true reason. He put down what he was holding and said, all right. And she smiled at him for the first time that morning and said, \"Good. Now help me, because I have never packed anything in my life and I have no idea what one takes.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 7","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","7 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-7-4":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|7","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":7},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:6:cards:4:text","title":"The Ramayana · I Am Coming With You","body":"Lakshmana had been standing in the doorway for some while with his bow already on his shoulder, in the manner of a man who has heard the entire argument and does not intend to have one of his own. \"I am coming,\" he said. \"That is not a question, so do not answer it as if it were.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 7","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","7 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-7-5":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|7","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":7},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:6:cards:5:text","title":"The Ramayana · I Am Coming With You","body":"Urmila, his wife, stayed. This gets left out of most tellings and should not be: she said goodbye to her husband on the day after her sister's life ended and stayed in that palace for the whole fourteen years, and there are poets in more than one language who have written her side of it.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 7","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","7 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-7-7":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|7","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":7},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:6:cards:7:text","title":"The Ramayana · I Am Coming With You","body":"And then the city did something nobody had planned for. It followed them. Not a delegation — the city. Old men, cooks, children, cattle, entire streets walking out of the gate behind the chariot, saying if Ayodhya is where Rama is then this is Ayodhya now.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 7","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","7 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-7-8":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|7","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":7},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:6:cards:8:text","title":"The Ramayana · I Am Coming With You","body":"They camped that night by the river. Rama waited until everyone was asleep, and then woke Sumantra the charioteer, and they drove away in the dark with the wheels muffled — because he knew that if he asked those people to go home in daylight they would refuse, and he could not do that to them twice.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 7","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","7 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-7-9":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|7","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":7},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:6:cards:9:text","title":"The Ramayana · I Am Coming With You","body":"At the Ganga a boatman named Guha came down to meet them, a friend from the old days, king of his own river people. He wept. He offered them his house, his food, his boats, his kingdom. Rama took the boat.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 7","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","7 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-7-10":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|7","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":7},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:6:cards:10:text","title":"The Ramayana · I Am Coming With You","body":"Before he got in, Rama said goodbye to Sumantra and told him what to say at home, and Sumantra said, \"I have driven you since you were four years old and I am not able to remember a single word of it.\" He remembered every word.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 7","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","7 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-8-1":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|8","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":8},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:7:cards:1:text","title":"The Ramayana · Bharata and the Sandals","body":"Messengers went to fetch Bharata and were told to say nothing on the road. So he rode four days back to Ayodhya with a bad feeling and no information, and found the city with its doors shut and no smoke from any kitchen, and went straight to his mother's rooms because that is where you go.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 8","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","8 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-8-2":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|8","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":8},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:7:cards:2:text","title":"The Ramayana · Bharata and the Sandals","body":"Kaikeyi told him. She told it as good news. She had rehearsed it as good news. She got as far as \"and so tomorrow you will be crowned\" and stopped, because of what was happening to her son's face.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 8","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","8 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-8-3":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|8","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":8},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:7:cards:3:text","title":"The Ramayana · Bharata and the Sandals","body":"\"Where is my father.\" \"He is dead.\" \"Where is Rama.\" \"In the forest. Fourteen years.\" Bharata sat down on the floor of his mother's room, and then said the thing that broke her: \"What did I ever do to make you think I wanted this?\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 8","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","8 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-8-4":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|8","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":8},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:7:cards:4:text","title":"The Ramayana · Bharata and the Sandals","body":"He said a great deal more, none of it kind, and Kaikeyi took every word of it, and when he had finished she said, \"I did it for you,\" and he said, \"You did it and gave it my name. That is not the same.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 8","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","8 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-8-5":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|8","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":8},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:7:cards:5:text","title":"The Ramayana · Bharata and the Sandals","body":"He did the funeral rites, because there was nobody else to do them. Then he stood up in the court, in front of every minister who had been waiting to crown him, and said, \"I am not the king. There is a king. He is barefoot in a forest, and I am going to fetch him.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 8","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","8 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-8-7":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|8","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":8},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:7:cards:7:text","title":"The Ramayana · Bharata and the Sandals","body":"Lakshmana, up in the rocks at Chitrakuta, saw them coming too, and reached for his bow and started saying things. Rama put a hand on his arm. \"That is Bharata,\" he said. \"Look at how he is walking. Put it down.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 8","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","8 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-8-8":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|8","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":8},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:7:cards:8:text","title":"The Ramayana · Bharata and the Sandals","body":"Bharata came up the last of the hill on foot with his shoes in his hand, and saw his brother in bark cloth with his hair matted like a hermit's, and did not manage to say any of the speech he had prepared. He got out the word \"Father\" and then nothing.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 8","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","8 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-8-9":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|8","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":8},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:7:cards:9:text","title":"The Ramayana · Bharata and the Sandals","body":"They talked for two days. Bharata argued every way there is: that the promise was extracted, that a dead man's word cannot bind the living, that the country needed him, that it was his fault, that it was nobody's fault. Rama listened to all of it properly, and at the end of it said, \"I gave my word too. Fourteen years. I have thirteen and a half left.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 8","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","8 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-8-10":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|8","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":8},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:7:cards:10:text","title":"The Ramayana · Bharata and the Sandals","body":"So Bharata gave up and asked for one thing instead. He took the wooden sandals off his brother's feet, put them on his own head, and carried them back down the hill in front of the whole city, and nobody said a word the entire way.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 8","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","8 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-9-1":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|9","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":9},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:8:cards:1:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Years in the Forest","body":"They learned things nobody in a palace knows. Which leaves hold water. How to tell four hours before rain. Which of the deer will come and take food out of your hand, and which one only pretends she will.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 9","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","9 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-9-2":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|9","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":9},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:8:cards:2:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Years in the Forest","body":"An old sage called Agastya, who lived furthest south of anyone, sent them on to a place called Panchavati on the Godavari river — five banyan trees, a bend in the water, and a view of the hills that Lakshmana said was better than the one from the palace roof.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 9","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","9 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-9-3":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|9","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":9},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:8:cards:3:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Years in the Forest","body":"Lakshmana built the hut. He was very serious about it. He cut and levelled and lashed and re-did the roof twice, and when it was finished he stood back and would not say anything, and Rama walked round it and said, \"This is the best thing anyone in our family has ever made,\" and Lakshmana had to go and look at the river for a bit.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 9","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","9 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-9-4":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|9","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":9},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:8:cards:4:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Years in the Forest","body":"They were happy. It has to be said clearly, because the story is about to become something else: those were good years. Sita planted things. Rama and Lakshmana hunted and came back and argued about who had seen the bird first. In the evenings the three of them sat outside and talked nonsense until it got dark.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 9","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","9 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-9-6":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|9","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":9},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:8:cards:6:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Years in the Forest","body":"And here Sita said a thing that she is not given nearly enough credit for. She had been watching Rama sharpen arrows for three evenings running, and she put down what she was doing and said, \"May I argue with you about something?\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 9","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","9 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-9-7":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|9","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":9},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:8:cards:7:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Years in the Forest","body":"\"We came here to live like hermits. A hermit does not carry a weapon. And a person who carries a weapon every day, in the end, finds a use for it — that is what weapons are for. I am not telling you what to do. I am telling you I have thought about it and it worries me.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 9","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","9 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-9-8":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|9","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":9},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:8:cards:8:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Years in the Forest","body":"Rama did not brush it off. He thought about it for a while. \"You are right that it is a danger,\" he said. \"But those people came to us and asked. I would rather be a man who is dangerous and keeps his promise than a man who is safe and did not. If I am wrong, you will be the one who told me first.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 9","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","9 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-9-9":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|9","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":9},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:8:cards:9:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Years in the Forest","body":"\"Yes,\" said Sita. \"I will be. Constantly.\" And that was the whole quarrel, and they went in to supper.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 9","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","9 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-11-1":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|11","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":11},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:10:cards:1:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Golden Deer","body":"\"No,\" said Maricha. \"Listen to me as a friend, because nobody else will say it. Leave those three alone. He is not a boy with a bow. And what you are describing is not a raid, it is a theft, and it will finish you and your city and everyone in it.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 11","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","11 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-11-2":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|11","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":11},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:10:cards:2:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Golden Deer","body":"\"You will do it,\" said Ravana, \"or I will do worse than he ever did.\" And Maricha looked at him for a while and said, \"Then I would rather die at Rama's hands than at yours,\" which is the closest thing to an argument anybody won with Ravana that year.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 11","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","11 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-11-3":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|11","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":11},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:10:cards:3:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Golden Deer","body":"The next morning there was a deer at the edge of the clearing that was made of gold. Not gold-coloured. Gold, with silver spots that moved like water, and horns like little branches of coral, and it walked out into the open and looked at Sita and then away.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 11","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","11 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-11-4":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|11","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":11},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:10:cards:4:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Golden Deer","body":"\"Rama,\" she said. \"Come and look at this.\" He came. Lakshmana came. All three of them stood and stared at a thing that could not exist. \"I want it,\" said Sita. \"Not to keep. I want to know what it is. There is no such animal. Look at it and tell me there is.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 11","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","11 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-11-6":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|11","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":11},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:10:cards:6:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Golden Deer","body":"He went after it. It led him a long way — out of the clearing, over the ridge, across a stream and up into country he did not know, always about forty paces ahead, always stopping to let him catch up. Which is when he stopped believing in it.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 11","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","11 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-11-7":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|11","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":11},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:10:cards:7:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Golden Deer","body":"Maricha, when the arrow found him, did the last thing he had been told to do, and did it well. He called out, in Rama's exact voice, loud enough to carry the whole way back over the ridge: \"Sita! Lakshmana! Help me!\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 11","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","11 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-11-8":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|11","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":11},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:10:cards:8:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Golden Deer","body":"Sita was up before it finished. Lakshmana did not move. \"That is not him.\" \"It is his voice.\" \"It is his voice being used. Nothing in this forest can hurt him.\" And Sita — frightened, and angry, and certain — said a thing to Lakshmana so cruel that she would spend the rest of her life trying to take it back: that perhaps he was standing there hoping.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 11","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","11 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-11-9":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|11","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":11},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:10:cards:9:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Golden Deer","body":"He went white. He picked up his bow, and bowed to her, and said, \"I hope you never remember saying that,\" and walked out of the clearing. In many tellings — Tulsidas's among them, and it is the one most children in the north grow up with — he stops at the edge and draws a line in the dust and tells her not to cross it.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 11","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","11 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-12-1":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|12","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":12},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:11:cards:1:text","title":"The Ramayana · Jatayu","body":"He heard her before he saw anything. So he came down out of the sky at a flying chariot at a speed he had no business flying at, an old bird with white round his eyes, shouting, \"PUT HER DOWN. I know your name and I know your father's name and I am telling you to PUT HER DOWN.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 12","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","12 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-12-2":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|12","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":12},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:11:cards:2:text","title":"The Ramayana · Jatayu","body":"They fought in the air over the forest. He broke the chariot. He tore the bow out of Ravana's hands. For a few minutes an old bird was winning — and then a sword took his wing off at the shoulder and he came down through the trees.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 12","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","12 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-12-3":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|12","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":12},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:11:cards:3:text","title":"The Ramayana · Jatayu","body":"She was going up and away south and she could see him lying in the branches getting smaller. And Sita did the only thing left that she could possibly do: she took off her jewellery, piece by piece, tied it in her scarf, and dropped it onto the top of a hill where five monkeys were sitting looking up.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 12","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","12 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-12-4":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|12","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":12},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:11:cards:4:text","title":"The Ramayana · Jatayu","body":"Rama came back over the ridge with the deer's body slung over his shoulder and met Lakshmana coming the other way, and neither of them said anything at all, and then they both began to run.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 12","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","12 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-12-6":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|12","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":12},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:11:cards:6:text","title":"The Ramayana · Jatayu","body":"What Rama did next is the part that people do not always tell, and it is the truest part. He went to pieces. He went round the clearing calling her name. He asked the trees whether they had seen her. He asked the river. He asked a deer, out loud, seriously, twice. Lakshmana followed him about and did not try to stop him.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 12","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","12 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-12-7":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|12","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":12},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:11:cards:7:text","title":"The Ramayana · Jatayu","body":"They found Jatayu at the bottom of the hill in the afternoon, still alive, only just, in a great heap of broken feathers. Rama went down beside him in the leaves and lifted the old bird's head into his lap.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 12","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","12 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-12-8":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|12","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":12},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:11:cards:8:text","title":"The Ramayana · Jatayu","body":"\"I could not hold him,\" said Jatayu. \"I am so sorry. I could not hold him.\" \"You went up against that,\" said Rama. \"At your age.\" \"I knew your father,\" said Jatayu, as if that explained everything, which it did.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 12","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","12 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-12-9":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|12","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":12},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:11:cards:9:text","title":"The Ramayana · Jatayu","body":"\"His name is Ravana,\" he said. \"Lanka. Across the sea. He went — \" and he could not lift the wing he did not have any more, so he turned his head instead, and looked, and Rama looked where he was looking. \"South,\" said Jatayu. And then he died, in the lap of a king's son, in the leaves.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 12","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","12 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-13-1":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|13","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":13},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:12:cards:1:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Berries Shabari Kept","body":"At the top was a hut and a very old woman with almost no teeth left, and when she saw the two of them come round the corner she put both hands over her mouth and sat down on the ground.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 13","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","13 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-13-2":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|13","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":13},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:12:cards:2:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Berries Shabari Kept","body":"Her name was Shabari. She was a forest woman who had lived with a community of hermits when she was young, and when her teacher was dying he had said one thing to her: stay here; one day Rama will come up that path.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 13","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","13 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-13-3":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|13","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":13},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:12:cards:3:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Berries Shabari Kept","body":"That had been a very long time ago. Everybody else had died. She had swept the path every single morning since, and laid the flowers, and gone to bed, and got up and swept it again, and told anybody who asked that he was coming.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 13","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","13 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-13-4":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|13","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":13},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:12:cards:4:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Berries Shabari Kept","body":"\"Sit,\" she said. \"Sit down, sit down, I have — \" and she went into the hut and came out with a leaf-plate of little wild berries, ber, the sour-sweet kind that grow on thorn bushes, and she was shaking so much she nearly dropped it.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 13","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","13 of 24"]]},"en-ramayana-13-5":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana|13","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":13},"src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:12:cards:5:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Berries Shabari Kept","body":"And then, in front of them, she picked one up, bit it, made a face, and threw it over her shoulder. Then another. Bit it, nodded, and put that one on the plate. Then another. Bit it, threw it away.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Ramayana, night 13","facts":[["Epic","The Ramayana"],["Night","13 of 24"]]}});
